(()=>{const e={today:'<div class="gm-hrow"><h2 class="gm-h2" id="td-title">Today across markets</h2><span class="gm-note" id="td-asof" data-source="today_moves"></span></div>\n<div id="td-quad" class="td-quad"></div>\n<div class="td-lead">\n<h3 class="gm-h4" id="td-sum-h">What changed</h3>\n<ul id="td-sum" data-source="today_summary"><li>—</li></ul>\n<h3 class="gm-h4" id="td-flags-h">Most unusual moves</h3>\n<ul id="td-flags" data-source="today_flags"><li>—</li></ul>\n</div>\n<div class="td-2">\n<div>\n<h3 class="gm-h3" id="td-ahead-h">Looking ahead: the next ten days</h3>\n<div class="td-filters" id="td-ahead-f"><span class="gm-seg" id="td-f-kind"></span><span class="gm-seg" id="td-f-imp"></span><span class="gm-seg" id="td-f-reg"></span><span class="gm-seg" id="td-f-win"></span><span class="gm-seg" id="td-f-size" title="Earnings: company size"></span><input id="td-f-q" type="search" placeholder="Search events" aria-label="Search events"><button type="button" class="gm-linkbtn" id="td-f-reset">Reset</button><span class="gm-note" id="td-f-count"></span></div>\n<div id="td-ahead" data-source="today_ahead">—</div>\n</div>\n<div>\n<h3 class="gm-h3" id="td-themes-h">Themes and crowding</h3>\n<ul class="td-list" id="td-themes" data-source="call_index positioning today_moves"><li>—</li></ul>\n<h3 class="gm-h3" id="td-rep-h">Large companies that reported this past week</h3>\n<div id="td-reported" data-source="today_earnings">—</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<h3 class="gm-h3" id="td-grid-h">Every market, every horizon</h3>\n<p class="td-key">Shading shows how unusual each move is against that asset’s own daily moves over the past year:<span class="td-up1"></span>1 sd<span class="td-up2"></span>2 sd<span class="td-up3"></span>3 sd or more, green up and red down. Yields and the curve move in basis points. Futures are front-month contracts, so a roll can show as a jump.</p>\n<div id="td-grid" data-source="today_moves">—</div>\n<h3 class="gm-h3" id="td-news-h">Latest headlines</h3>\n<ul class="td-list" id="td-news" data-source="today_news"><li>—</li></ul>',sb:'<div class="gm-hrow"><h2 class="gm-h2">Stocks vs bonds<button type="button" class="gm-i" data-info="sbTab" aria-label="About the stocks vs bonds model">i</button></h2></div>\n<div class="gm-grid2">\n<div class="gm-block" id="sb-call" data-source="model_sb">\n<div class="gm-xsbig" id="sb-big">—</div>\n<p class="gm-xsst" id="sb-sub"></p>\n<div id="sb-gauge" style="min-height:46px"></div>\n<ul class="gm-bul" id="sb-why"></ul>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block">\n<h3 class="gm-h3">Pillars</h3>\n<div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-compact gm-static" id="sb-pillars"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block"><h3 class="gm-h3">Equity weight over time</h3><div class="gm-chart" id="sb-hist" data-source="model_sb" style="min-height:240px"></div></div>\n<div class="gm-grid2">\n<div class="gm-block"><h3 class="gm-h3">What would change the call</h3><div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-compact gm-static" id="sb-trig" data-source="model_sb"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div><p class="gm-note">Each input moved on its own, others held where they are. &ldquo;Out of reach&rdquo; means the input would need to go beyond anything plausible.</p></div>\n</div>\n<p class="gm-note"><button type="button" class="gm-linkbtn" data-go="sbbt">Backtest, results by backdrop and live record</button></p>',firv:'<div class="gm-hrow"><h2 class="gm-h2">Fixed income relative value<button type="button" class="gm-i" data-info="frTab" aria-label="About the fixed income model">i</button></h2></div>\n<ul class="gm-bul" id="fr-lead" data-source="model_fi bond_stats"></ul>\n<div class="gm-block">\n<h3 class="gm-h3">Tilts against the US Aggregate</h3>\n<div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-compact gm-static" id="fr-levers"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-grid2">\n<div class="gm-block"><h3 class="gm-h3">Suggested portfolio</h3><div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-compact gm-static" id="fr-weights"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div>\n<p class="gm-note">Hold the Agg and apply these changes; each pair is funded from a fund of similar duration, so the overall duration stays close to the Agg&rsquo;s unless the duration lever is active.</p></div>\n<div class="gm-block"><div class="gm-hrow"><h3 class="gm-h3">Signal history</h3><select id="fr-lv"></select></div><div class="gm-chart" id="fr-hist" data-source="model_fi" style="min-height:240px"></div></div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-grid2">\n<div class="gm-block"><h3 class="gm-h3">What would change the call</h3><div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-compact gm-static" id="fr-trig" data-source="model_fi"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div><p class="gm-note">The level of each lever&rsquo;s main input at which the tilt would be neutral, and at which it would be at its full size.</p></div>\n</div>\n<p class="gm-note"><button type="button" class="gm-linkbtn" data-go="fibt">Backtest, results by backdrop and live record</button></p>',ra:'<div class="gm-hrow"><h2 class="gm-h2">Real assets, commodities and alternatives<button type="button" class="gm-i" data-info="raTab" aria-label="About the real assets model">i</button></h2></div>\n<ul class="gm-bul" id="ra-notes" data-source="model_ra"></ul>\n<div class="gm-block"><div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-compact gm-static" id="ra-table" data-source="model_ra"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div></div>\n<div class="gm-block"><div class="gm-hrow"><h3 class="gm-h3">Signal history</h3><select id="ra-sel"></select></div><div class="gm-chart" id="ra-hist" data-source="model_ra" style="min-height:220px"></div></div>\n<p class="gm-note"><button type="button" class="gm-linkbtn" data-go="rabt">Backtest of the real assets sleeve</button></p>\n<div class="gm-block" id="pm-block">\n<div class="gm-hrow"><h3 class="gm-h3">Private markets, through listed proxies<button type="button" class="gm-i" data-info="pmTab" aria-label="About the private markets calls">i</button></h3><button type="button" class="gm-linkbtn" data-go="reltrend">History on Relative trends</button></div>\n<ul class="gm-bul" id="pm-notes" data-source="model_pm"></ul>\n<div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-compact" id="pm-table" data-source="model_pm"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div>\n</div>',fx:'<div class="gm-hrow"><h2 class="gm-h2">Currencies<button type="button" class="gm-i" data-info="fxTab" aria-label="About the currencies page">i</button></h2><span class="gm-seg gm-per" id="fx-per"><button type="button" data-per="d1">1D</button><button type="button" data-per="w1">1W</button><button type="button" data-per="m1">1M</button><button type="button" data-per="m3">3M</button><button type="button" data-per="ytd">YTD</button><button type="button" data-per="m12">1Y</button><button type="button" data-per="y3">3Y</button><button type="button" data-per="y5">5Y</button><button type="button" data-per="y10">10Y</button></span></div>\n<ul class="gm-bul" id="fx-notes" data-source="fx_stats"></ul>\n<div class="gm-block"><h3 class="gm-h3" id="fx-chart-h">Change against the dollar</h3><div class="gm-chart" id="fx-chart" data-source="fx_stats fx_short"></div></div>\n<div class="gm-block"><div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-compact gm-static" id="fx-table" data-source="fx_stats fx_short"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div>\n<p class="gm-note">Changes are in dollars per unit of the currency, so a rise means the currency gained. PPP compares today&rsquo;s rate with the World Bank&rsquo;s purchasing-power-parity rate; positive means cheap. The suggested hedge ratio comes from the currency model: 50%, moving toward 0% when the model expects the currency to gain and toward 100% when it expects it to fall.</p></div>',overview:'<div class="gm-ovtop">\n<div class="gm-block gm-ovcol">\n<div class="gm-hrow"><h2 class="gm-h2">Equity market pulse</h2>\n<span class="gm-seg gm-per" id="ov-per"><button type="button" data-per="d1">1D</button><button type="button" data-per="wtd">WTD</button><button type="button" data-per="w1" aria-pressed="true">1W</button><button type="button" data-per="mtd">MTD</button><button type="button" data-per="m1">1M</button><button type="button" data-per="m3">3M</button><button type="button" data-per="qtd">QTD</button><button type="button" data-per="m6">6M</button><button type="button" data-per="ytd">YTD</button><button type="button" data-per="m12">1Y</button><button type="button" data-per="y2">2Y</button><button type="button" data-per="y3">3Y</button><button type="button" data-per="y5">5Y</button><button type="button" data-per="y10">10Y</button><button type="button" data-per="y15">15Y</button><button type="button" data-per="y20">20Y</button></span></div>\n<p class="gm-note" id="ov-per-note"></p>\n<ul class="gm-bul" id="ov-pulse" data-source="short_returns scores"></ul>\n<h3 class="gm-h3">Rates, dollar, credit, commodities</h3>\n<ul class="gm-bul" id="ov-macro" data-source="macro_live"></ul>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block gm-ovcol">\n<div class="gm-hrow"><h2 class="gm-h2">What&rsquo;s changing<button type="button" class="gm-i" data-info="emerging" aria-label="About what\'s changing">i</button></h2></div>\n<ul class="gm-em" id="ov-emerging" data-source="short_returns macro_live scores"></ul>\n<button type="button" class="gm-linkbtn" id="ov-emall" hidden>Show all</button>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block">\n<div class="gm-hrow"><h2 class="gm-h2">By category<button type="button" class="gm-i" data-info="catNotes" aria-label="About the category notes">i</button></h2></div>\n<div class="gm-cats" id="ov-cats" data-source="scores short_returns"></div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block" id="prefs">\n<h2 class="gm-h2">Style, size and region calls<button type="button" class="gm-i" data-info="prefBoard" aria-label="About the preference board">i</button></h2>\n<div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-static" id="pb-table" data-source="scores bt_pairs"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block" id="note">\n<h2 class="gm-h2">Weekly note<button type="button" class="gm-i" data-info="weeklyNote" aria-label="About the weekly note">i</button></h2>\n<p class="gm-note" id="note-date">Written at the Saturday rebuild under the default settings. Live sections above update with every price.</p>\n<div class="gm-prose gm-noteb" id="note-body" data-source="weekly_note"></div>\n</div>',short:'<div class="gm-block">\n<div class="gm-hrow"><h2 class="gm-h2">Short-term performance</h2>\n<span class="gm-seg gm-per" id="st-per"><button type="button" data-per="d1">1D</button><button type="button" data-per="wtd">WTD</button><button type="button" data-per="w1" aria-pressed="true">1W</button><button type="button" data-per="mtd">MTD</button><button type="button" data-per="m1">1M</button><button type="button" data-per="m3">3M</button><button type="button" data-per="qtd">QTD</button><button type="button" data-per="m6">6M</button><button type="button" data-per="ytd">YTD</button><button type="button" data-per="m12">1Y</button><button type="button" data-per="y2">2Y</button><button type="button" data-per="y3">3Y</button><button type="button" data-per="y5">5Y</button><button type="button" data-per="y10">10Y</button><button type="button" data-per="y15">15Y</button><button type="button" data-per="y20">20Y</button></span>\n<span class="gm-seg" id="st-rel"><button type="button" data-rel="abs" aria-pressed="true">Absolute</button><button type="button" data-rel="acwi">vs global equities</button></span></div>\n<ul class="gm-bul" id="st-pulse" data-source="short_returns scores"></ul>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-grid2">\n<div class="gm-block">\n<h3 class="gm-h3" id="st-chart-h">Return</h3>\n<div class="gm-chart" id="st-chart" data-source="short_returns scores"></div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block">\n<h3 class="gm-h3">By category, equal-weighted</h3>\n<div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-compact" id="st-cat" data-source="short_returns"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div>\n<h3 class="gm-h3">What&rsquo;s changing<button type="button" class="gm-i" data-info="emerging" aria-label="About what\'s changing">i</button></h3>\n<ul class="gm-em" id="st-emerging" data-source="short_returns macro_live scores"></ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block">\n<h2 class="gm-h2">Every sleeve<button type="button" class="gm-i" data-info="shortTable" aria-label="About this table">i</button></h2>\n<div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-compact" id="st-table" data-source="short_returns"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block">\n<h2 class="gm-h2">Rates, currencies, credit and commodities<button type="button" class="gm-i" data-info="macroLive" aria-label="About this table">i</button></h2>\n<div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-compact" id="st-macro" data-source="macro_live"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div>\n</div>',multi:'<div class="gm-block">\n<div class="gm-hrow"><h2 class="gm-h2">Multi-asset performance<button type="button" class="gm-i" data-info="maTab" aria-label="About multi-asset performance">i</button></h2>\n<span class="gm-seg gm-per" id="ma-per"><button type="button" data-per="d1">1D</button><button type="button" data-per="wtd">WTD</button><button type="button" data-per="w1" aria-pressed="true">1W</button><button type="button" data-per="mtd">MTD</button><button type="button" data-per="m1">1M</button><button type="button" data-per="m3">3M</button><button type="button" data-per="qtd">QTD</button><button type="button" data-per="m6">6M</button><button type="button" data-per="ytd">YTD</button><button type="button" data-per="m12">1Y</button><button type="button" data-per="y2">2Y</button><button type="button" data-per="y3">3Y</button><button type="button" data-per="y5">5Y</button><button type="button" data-per="y10">10Y</button><button type="button" data-per="y15">15Y</button><button type="button" data-per="y20">20Y</button></span></div>\n<p class="gm-note" id="ma-per-note"></p>\n<ul class="gm-bul" id="ma-notes" data-source="multi_stats multi_short multi_corr"></ul>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-grid2">\n<div class="gm-block">\n<h3 class="gm-h3" id="ma-chart-h">Return</h3>\n<div class="gm-legend" id="ma-legend"></div>\n<div class="gm-chart" id="ma-chart" data-source="multi_short multi_stats"></div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block">\n<h3 class="gm-h3" id="ma-scatter-h">Return against volatility</h3>\n<div class="gm-chart" id="ma-scatter" data-source="multi_short multi_stats" style="min-height:360px"></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block">\n<h2 class="gm-h2">Every asset, every horizon</h2>\n<div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-compact" id="ma-grid" data-source="multi_short multi_stats"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block">\n<h2 class="gm-h2">Calendar-year returns, ranked<button type="button" class="gm-i" data-info="maQuilt" aria-label="About this chart">i</button></h2>\n<div class="gm-tablewrap gm-quiltwrap" id="ma-quilt" data-source="multi_stats"></div>\n</div>',bonds:'<div class="gm-block">\n<div class="gm-hrow"><h2 class="gm-h2">Fixed income<button type="button" class="gm-i" data-info="fiTab" aria-label="About fixed income">i</button></h2>\n<span class="gm-seg gm-per" id="fi-per"><button type="button" data-per="d1">1D</button><button type="button" data-per="wtd">WTD</button><button type="button" data-per="w1" aria-pressed="true">1W</button><button type="button" data-per="mtd">MTD</button><button type="button" data-per="m1">1M</button><button type="button" data-per="m3">3M</button><button type="button" data-per="qtd">QTD</button><button type="button" data-per="m6">6M</button><button type="button" data-per="ytd">YTD</button><button type="button" data-per="m12">1Y</button><button type="button" data-per="y2">2Y</button><button type="button" data-per="y3">3Y</button><button type="button" data-per="y5">5Y</button><button type="button" data-per="y10">10Y</button><button type="button" data-per="y15">15Y</button><button type="button" data-per="y20">20Y</button></span></div>\n<p class="gm-note" id="fi-per-note"></p>\n<ul class="gm-bul" id="fi-notes" data-source="bond_stats bond_short curve_live"></ul>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-grid2">\n<div class="gm-block">\n<h3 class="gm-h3">US Treasury curve</h3>\n<div class="gm-chart" id="fi-curve" data-source="curve_live" style="min-height:300px"></div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block">\n<h3 class="gm-h3">Yields and changes</h3>\n<div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-compact" id="fi-curvet" data-source="curve_live"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block">\n<div class="gm-hrow"><h3 class="gm-h3">Curve history</h3>\n<span class="gm-seg" id="fi-hm"><button type="button" data-hm="recent" aria-pressed="true">Spreads, past year</button><button type="button" data-hm="long">Yields since 2005</button></span></div>\n<div class="gm-chart" id="fi-hist" data-source="curve_live" style="min-height:300px"></div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-grid2">\n<div class="gm-block">\n<h3 class="gm-h3" id="fi-chart-h">Return</h3>\n<div class="gm-chart" id="fi-chart" data-source="bond_short bond_stats"></div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block">\n<h3 class="gm-h3">Compare any two measures<button type="button" class="gm-i" data-info="fiCarry" aria-label="About this chart">i</button></h3>\n<div class="gm-inputs gm-fxctl">\n<label class="gm-field"><span class="gm-label">Horizontal</span><select id="fi-x"></select></label>\n<button type="button" class="gm-linkbtn" id="fi-swap" aria-label="Swap axes">Swap</button>\n<label class="gm-field"><span class="gm-label">Vertical</span><select id="fi-y"></select></label>\n<label class="gm-check"><input type="checkbox" id="fi-fit" checked> Fit line</label>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-seg gm-presets" id="fi-presets"><button type="button" data-p="duration,ttmYield">Yield vs duration</button><button type="button" data-p="equityBeta,pickup">Spread vs equity risk</button><button type="button" data-p="vol3y,ann10y">10Y return vs risk</button><button type="button" data-p="pickup,x_m1">Spread vs 1M excess return</button><button type="button" data-p="duration,r_ytd">YTD vs duration</button></div>\n<div class="gm-legend" id="fi-carry-legend"></div>\n<div class="gm-chart" id="fi-carry" data-source="bond_stats" style="min-height:380px"></div>\n<p class="gm-note" id="fi-carry-note"></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block">\n<h2 class="gm-h2">Returns by sector, quality and maturity</h2>\n<div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-compact" id="fi-ret" data-source="bond_short bond_stats"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block">\n<h2 class="gm-h2">Yield, duration and risk<button type="button" class="gm-i" data-info="fiFund" aria-label="About this table">i</button></h2>\n<div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-compact" id="fi-fund" data-source="bond_stats"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div>\n</div>',outlook:'<div class="gm-block" id="scenario">\n<h2 class="gm-h2">Scenario lens<button type="button" class="gm-i" data-info="scenario" aria-label="About the scenario lens">i</button></h2>\n<div class="gm-inputs">\n<label class="gm-field"><span class="gm-label">Global equities, %</span><span class="gm-numin"><input id="sc-eq" type="number" step="1" value="0"></span></label>\n<label class="gm-field"><span class="gm-label">US 10-year yield, bp</span><span class="gm-numin"><input id="sc-bp" type="number" step="10" value="-50"></span></label>\n<label class="gm-field"><span class="gm-label">US dollar, %</span><span class="gm-numin"><input id="sc-usd" type="number" step="1" value="0"></span></label>\n<label class="gm-field"><span class="gm-label">Oil, %</span><span class="gm-numin"><input id="sc-oil" type="number" step="5" value="0"></span></label>\n<span class="gm-seg" id="sc-presets"><button type="button" data-s=\'{"bp":-50}\'>Rates −50bp</button><button type="button" data-s=\'{"bp":75}\'>Rates +75bp</button><button type="button" data-s=\'{"usd":-5}\'>Dollar −5%</button><button type="button" data-s=\'{"oil":25}\'>Oil +25%</button><button type="button" data-s=\'{"eq":-10}\'>Equities −10%</button></span>\n</div>\n<p class="gm-note" id="sc-note"></p>\n<div class="gm-chart" id="sc-chart" data-source="sensitivities"></div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block">\n<h2 class="gm-h2">Across all assets</h2>\n<p class="gm-note">The same shocks applied to every fund the models use, from five years of monthly sensitivities, and to the model portfolio against its reference.</p>\n<div class="gm-grid2">\n<div class="gm-chart" id="sc2-chart" data-source="risk_model"></div>\n<div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-compact gm-static" id="sc2-port" data-source="risk_model"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block">\n<h2 class="gm-h2">Replay a backdrop<button type="button" class="gm-i" data-info="scRegime" aria-label="About this view">i</button></h2>\n<p class="gm-note">How each asset and the model portfolio did, on average, in past months that were in each growth and inflation state. Unlike the shocks above, this carries the full mix of moves that come with a backdrop.</p>\n<div class="gm-hrow"><span class="gm-seg" id="sc3-state"></span></div>\n<div class="gm-grid2">\n<div class="gm-chart" id="sc3-chart" data-source="regime_playbook"></div>\n<div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-compact gm-static" id="sc3-port" data-source="regime_playbook"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div>\n</div>\n</div>',deploy:'<div class="gm-block">\n<h2 class="gm-h2">Deploy cash<button type="button" class="gm-i" data-info="deploy" aria-label="About deployment rules">i</button></h2>\n<p class="gm-note">Where to put new money to work for a 12 to 36 month hold, across equity sleeves, bond funds and real assets. Equities follow the weights, sentiment reading and category filter set above.</p>\n<div class="gm-hrow gm-deprow"><span class="gm-seg" id="d-mode"><button type="button" data-mode="overall" aria-pressed="true">Top overall</button><button type="button" data-mode="cls">Per asset class</button><button type="button" data-mode="region">Per equity region</button><button type="button" data-mode="category">Per equity category</button><button type="button" data-mode="bond">Per bond sector</button></span>\n<span class="gm-seg" id="d-split"><button type="button" data-split="score" aria-pressed="true">Split by signal strength</button><button type="button" data-split="model">Follow the model portfolio mix</button></span></div>\n<div class="gm-inputs">\n<label class="gm-field"><span class="gm-label">Amount ($)</span><span class="gm-numin"><input id="d-amt" type="number" value="5000" min="0" max="100000000" step="500"></span></label>\n<label class="gm-field gm-dep-n"><span class="gm-label">Number of picks</span><span class="gm-numin"><input id="d-n" type="number" value="3" min="1" max="12" step="1"></span></label>\n<label class="gm-field gm-dep-per" hidden><span class="gm-label">Picks per slice</span><span class="gm-numin"><input id="d-per" type="number" value="1" min="1" max="4" step="1"></span></label>\n<label class="gm-field"><span class="gm-label">Minimum score</span><span class="gm-numin"><input id="d-min" type="number" value="55" min="0" max="100" step="1"></span></label>\n<label class="gm-field"><span class="gm-label">Maximum correlation<button type="button" class="gm-i" data-info="maxCorr" aria-label="About maximum correlation">i</button></span><span class="gm-numin"><input id="d-corr" type="number" value="0.85" min="0" max="1" step="0.05"></span></label>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-inputs">\n<label class="gm-check"><input type="checkbox" id="d-eq" checked> Equities</label>\n<label class="gm-check"><input type="checkbox" id="d-fi" checked> Fixed income</label>\n<label class="gm-check"><input type="checkbox" id="d-ra" checked> Real assets</label>\n<label class="gm-check"><input type="checkbox" id="d-full" checked> Equities need fundamentals and valuation</label>\n<label class="gm-check"><input type="checkbox" id="d-glob" checked> Exclude global blends (ACWI, ACWX)</label>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-tablewrap">\n<table class="gm-table gm-static" id="picks" data-source="scores model_fi model_ra bond_stats"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table>\n</div>\n<dl class="gm-takeaways" id="deploy-notes" data-source="scores model_fi model_ra"></dl>\n<div class="gm-block" id="clusters">\n<h2 class="gm-h2">Equity sleeves: how many separate bets?<button type="button" class="gm-i" data-info="clusters" aria-label="About the clusters">i</button></h2>\n<p class="gm-note" id="cl-note"></p>\n<div class="gm-chart" id="cl-chart" data-source="correlations"></div>\n</div>',visuals:'<div class="gm-block" id="robust">\n<h2 class="gm-h2">Strength against robustness<button type="button" class="gm-i" data-info="robustMap" aria-label="About this chart">i</button></h2>\n<p class="gm-note">Composite under the current settings against conviction: how often each sleeve lands in the top fifth (above zero) or the bottom fifth (below zero) across all 70 settings. Calls in the corners are the ones that do not depend on the settings you chose.</p>\n<div class="gm-chart" id="rm-chart" data-source="scores"></div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block" id="trail">\n<h2 class="gm-h2">Where sleeves are heading<button type="button" class="gm-i" data-info="trailChart" aria-label="About this chart">i</button></h2>\n<p class="gm-note">Valuation against technicals, with each sleeve\'s path over the last three month-ends from the rebuilt history and the arrow at today\'s live score. With the whole universe selected, the six highest and six lowest composites are shown; choose a group under Universe in the equity settings to see all of its sleeves.</p>\n<div class="gm-chart" id="tc-chart" data-source="sleeve_series scores" style="min-height:380px"></div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block" id="ownstrip">\n<h2 class="gm-h2">Cycle-adjusted P/E within each sleeve\'s own range</h2>\n<p class="gm-note">Where today\'s cycle-adjusted P/E sits in the sleeve\'s own monthly record: left is the cheapest it has been, right the dearest. This is the default valuation read.</p>\n<div class="gm-chart" id="os-chart" data-source="scores val_history"></div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block">\n<h2 class="gm-h2">Score heatmap</h2>\n<p class="gm-note">Every sleeve on every pillar. Sort with the control in the equity settings; by Category groups sleeves under their category.</p>\n<div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-static gm-hm" id="vh-heat" data-source="scores"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block">\n<h2 class="gm-h2">Category averages</h2>\n<div class="gm-inputs">\n<label class="gm-field"><span class="gm-label">Categories</span><span class="gm-seg" id="ca-kind"><button type="button" data-kind="group" aria-pressed="true">Groups</button><button type="button" data-kind="region" aria-pressed="false">Regions</button></span></label>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-chart" id="ca-chart" data-source="scores"></div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block">\n<h2 class="gm-h2">Four-box matrix</h2>\n<div class="gm-inputs">\n<label class="gm-field"><span class="gm-label">Across</span><select id="q-x"><option value="valuation">Valuation</option><option value="fundamentals">Fundamentals</option><option value="technicals">Technicals</option><option value="sentiment">Sentiment</option><option value="composite">Composite</option></select></label>\n<label class="gm-field"><span class="gm-label">Up</span><select id="q-y"><option value="fundamentals">Fundamentals</option><option value="valuation">Valuation</option><option value="technicals">Technicals</option><option value="sentiment">Sentiment</option><option value="composite">Composite</option></select></label>\n<label class="gm-field"><span class="gm-label">Color</span><select id="q-c"><option value="technicals">Technicals</option><option value="fundamentals">Fundamentals</option><option value="valuation">Valuation</option><option value="sentiment">Sentiment</option><option value="composite">Composite</option><option value="category">Category</option></select></label>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-map" id="quad-chart" data-source="scores"></div>\n<div class="gm-quads" id="quads" data-source="scores"></div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block">\n<h2 class="gm-h2">Metric explorer</h2>\n<p class="gm-note">Plot any two metrics across the sleeves, with a least-squares line. Sleeves far above or below the line are where one metric is out of step with the other.</p>\n<div class="gm-inputs">\n<label class="gm-field"><span class="gm-label">Across</span><select id="m-x"></select></label>\n<label class="gm-field"><span class="gm-label">Up</span><select id="m-y"></select></label>\n<label class="gm-field"><span class="gm-label">Highlight</span><select id="m-h"></select></label>\n<label class="gm-field"><span class="gm-label">Color by</span><span class="gm-seg" id="m-colorby"><button type="button" data-cb="group" aria-pressed="true">Group</button><button type="button" data-cb="region" aria-pressed="false">Region</button></span></label>\n<label class="gm-check"><input type="checkbox" id="m-percat"> A line for each category</label>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-chips" id="m-cats" aria-label="Categories shown"></div>\n<div class="gm-grid">\n<div class="gm-block"><div class="gm-map" id="mx-chart" data-source="scores relations" style="min-height:380px"></div></div>\n<div class="gm-block">\n<dl class="gm-takeaways" id="mx-notes" data-source="relations scores"></dl>\n<div class="gm-controls" style="justify-content: space-between"><h3 class="gm-h3">Strongest relationships<button type="button" class="gm-i" data-info="relations" aria-label="About relationships">i</button></h3>\n<label class="gm-check"><input type="checkbox" id="m-cross" checked> Across pillars only</label></div>\n<table class="gm-table gm-pt" id="rel" data-source="relations" data-row-key="pair"><thead><tr><th data-field="x">Metric A</th><th data-field="y">Metric B</th><th data-field="corr">Correlation</th><th data-field="r2">R²</th></tr></thead><tbody></tbody></table>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block">\n<h2 class="gm-h2">Composite ranking</h2>\n<p class="gm-note" id="rank-note">—</p>\n<div class="gm-chart" id="rank-chart" data-source="scores"></div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block">\n<h2 class="gm-h2">Score distribution</h2>\n<p class="gm-note">Each dot is a sleeve; the line marks the median. Wide spreads mean a pillar is separating the sleeves sharply.</p>\n<div class="gm-chart" id="dist-chart" data-source="scores"></div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block">\n<h2 class="gm-h2">Pillar profiles</h2>\n<div class="gm-inputs">\n<label class="gm-field"><span class="gm-label">Sleeve 1</span><select id="pp-1"></select></label>\n<label class="gm-field"><span class="gm-label">Sleeve 2</span><select id="pp-2"></select></label>\n<label class="gm-field"><span class="gm-label">Sleeve 3</span><select id="pp-3"></select></label>\n<label class="gm-field"><span class="gm-label">Sleeve 4</span><select id="pp-4"></select></label>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-chart" id="pp-chart" data-source="scores"></div>\n</div>',breadth:'<div class="gm-block">\n<h2 class="gm-h2">Breadth<button type="button" class="gm-i" data-info="breadthTab" aria-label="About breadth">i</button></h2>\n<p class="gm-note">How many of each fund\'s holdings are participating, from live quotes on every holding in the look-through, weighted by fund weight.</p>\n<dl class="gm-takeaways" id="br-notes" data-source="breadth"></dl>\n<div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-static gm-hm" id="br-table" data-source="breadth"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block">\n<div class="gm-inputs"><label class="gm-field"><span class="gm-label">Chart</span><select id="br-m"><option value="above200d">Holdings above 200-day average</option><option value="above50d">Holdings above 50-day average</option><option value="netHighLow">Near 52-week highs minus near lows</option><option value="upToday">Holdings up today</option></select></label></div>\n<div class="gm-chart" id="br-chart" data-source="breadth"></div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block">\n<h2 class="gm-h2">Concentration<button type="button" class="gm-i" data-info="concTab" aria-label="About concentration">i</button></h2>\n<p class="gm-note">How much each fund depends on a few names, sectors and countries. Effective holdings is the number of equal-weight positions that would give the same concentration.</p>\n<dl class="gm-takeaways" id="cn-notes" data-source="concentration"></dl>\n<div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-static gm-hm" id="cn-table" data-source="concentration"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block">\n<div class="gm-inputs"><label class="gm-field"><span class="gm-label">Chart</span><select id="cn-m"><option value="top10Weight">Top-10 weight</option><option value="topWeight">Largest holding weight</option><option value="effectiveHoldings">Effective holdings</option><option value="topSectorWeight">Largest sector weight</option><option value="effectiveSectors">Effective sectors</option></select></label></div>\n<div class="gm-chart" id="cn-chart" data-source="concentration"></div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block">\n<h2 class="gm-h2">Sector exposure</h2>\n<p class="gm-note">Look-through sector weights as a share of the holdings covered. Darker blue is a larger weight.</p>\n<div class="gm-tablewrap"><div class="gm-heat gm-sheat" id="sx-heat" data-source="sector_exposure"></div></div>\n</div>',returns:'<template id="tpl-returns">\n<div class="gm-block">\n<h2 class="gm-h2">Historical returns</h2>\n<p class="gm-note">Annualized total returns from dividend-adjusted prices, ending at today\'s live price. Sleeves without enough history show a dash.</p>\n<div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-static gm-hm" id="lr-table" data-source="long_returns"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block">\n<h2 class="gm-h2">Growth of 100</h2>\n<div class="gm-inputs">\n<label class="gm-field"><span class="gm-label">Sleeve 1</span><select id="g-1"></select></label>\n<label class="gm-field"><span class="gm-label">Sleeve 2</span><select id="g-2"></select></label>\n<label class="gm-field"><span class="gm-label">Sleeve 3</span><select id="g-3"></select></label>\n<label class="gm-field"><span class="gm-label">Sleeve 4</span><select id="g-4"></select></label>\n<label class="gm-field"><span class="gm-label">Range</span><span class="gm-seg" id="g-range"><button type="button" data-y="1">1Y</button><button type="button" data-y="3">3Y</button><button type="button" data-y="5">5Y</button><button type="button" data-y="10" aria-pressed="true">10Y</button><button type="button" data-y="15">15Y</button><button type="button" data-y="20">20Y</button></span></label>\n<label class="gm-check"><input type="checkbox" id="g-log"> Log scale</label>\n</div>\n<div data-own-range class="gm-chart" id="g-chart" data-source="etf_weekly" style="min-height:320px"></div>\n<dl class="gm-takeaways" id="g-notes" data-source="etf_weekly"></dl>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block">\n<h2 class="gm-h2">Calendar-year returns</h2>\n<p class="gm-note">The current year runs to today. Green is a gain, red a loss; stronger color is a larger move.</p>\n<div class="gm-tablewrap"><div class="gm-heat gm-cheat" id="cal-heat" data-source="calendar_returns"></div></div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block">\n<h2 class="gm-h2">Monthly returns</h2>\n<div class="gm-inputs"><label class="gm-field"><span class="gm-label">Sleeve</span><select id="mc-s"></select></label></div>\n<div class="gm-tablewrap"><div class="gm-heat gm-cheat" id="mc-heat" data-source="etf_weekly"></div></div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block">\n<h2 class="gm-h2">Recent returns</h2>\n<div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-static gm-hm" id="rr-table" data-source="scores"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block">\n<h2 class="gm-h2">Relative strength<button type="button" class="gm-i" data-info="relStrength" aria-label="About relative strength">i</button></h2>\n<div class="gm-inputs">\n<label class="gm-field"><span class="gm-label">Sleeve 1</span><select id="t-1"></select></label>\n<label class="gm-field"><span class="gm-label">Sleeve 2</span><select id="t-2"></select></label>\n<label class="gm-field"><span class="gm-label">Sleeve 3</span><select id="t-3"></select></label>\n<label class="gm-field"><span class="gm-label">Against</span><select id="t-b"></select></label>\n<label class="gm-field"><span class="gm-label">Window</span><span class="gm-seg" id="t-win"><button type="button" data-win="12" aria-pressed="false">12M</button><button type="button" data-win="24" aria-pressed="false">24M</button><button type="button" data-win="36" aria-pressed="true">36M</button></span></label>\n</div>\n<div data-own-range class="gm-chart" id="rs-chart" data-source="monthly" style="min-height:320px"></div>\n<p class="gm-note" id="rs-note">—</p>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block">\n<h2 class="gm-h2">Correlation of monthly returns, 36 months<button type="button" class="gm-i" data-info="corr" aria-label="About correlation">i</button></h2>\n<p class="gm-note">Pale cells are the diversifiers. The universe filter above narrows the matrix; select a sleeve to list its best and worst diversifiers.</p>\n<div class="gm-inputs"><label class="gm-field"><span class="gm-label">Diversifiers for</span><select id="c-s"></select></label></div>\n<dl class="gm-takeaways" id="c-notes" data-source="correlations"></dl>\n<div class="gm-tablewrap"><div class="gm-heat" id="heat" data-source="correlations"></div></div>\n</div>\n</template>',series:'<template id="tpl-series">\n<div class="gm-block">\n<h2 class="gm-h2">Time series explorer<button type="button" class="gm-i" data-info="seriesTab" aria-label="About the time series">i</button></h2>\n<p class="gm-note">Any metric or valuation multiple over time, for a sleeve, a category of sleeves or a single holding. With one series, the shaded band is its own average plus or minus one standard deviation, so you can see whether today is rich or cheap against its own history.</p>\n<div class="gm-inputs">\n<label class="gm-field"><span class="gm-label">Metric</span><select id="ts-m"></select></label>\n<label class="gm-field"><span class="gm-label">Range</span><span class="gm-seg" id="ts-range"><button type="button" data-y="1">1Y</button><button type="button" data-y="3">3Y</button><button type="button" data-y="5">5Y</button><button type="button" data-y="10" aria-pressed="true">10Y</button><button type="button" data-y="15">15Y</button><button type="button" data-y="20">20Y</button></span></label>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-series-pick" id="ts-picks"></div>\n<div data-own-range class="gm-chart" id="ts-chart" data-source="sleeve_series group_series constituent_series" style="min-height:360px"></div>\n<div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-static" id="ts-stats" data-source="sleeve_series group_series constituent_series"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block">\n<h2 class="gm-h2">Where multiples sit against their own history</h2>\n<p class="gm-note">For each sleeve, today\'s reconstructed trailing P/E against its range since 2011: the bar spans the lowest to highest month, the tick is the median and the dot is today.</p>\n<div class="gm-inputs"><label class="gm-field"><span class="gm-label">Multiple</span><select id="rh-m"><option value="peTTM">Trailing P/E</option><option value="pb">Price to book</option><option value="evEbitda">EV to EBITDA</option><option value="fcfYield">Free cash flow yield</option><option value="divYield">Dividend yield</option></select></label></div>\n<div class="gm-chart" id="rh-chart" data-source="sleeve_series"></div>\n</div>\n</template>',allocation:'<div class="gm-block" id="backdrop">\n<h2 class="gm-h2">Equities against bonds, and the backdrop<button type="button" class="gm-i" data-info="erp" aria-label="About the equity risk premium">i</button></h2>\n<p class="gm-note">The cycle-adjusted earnings yield (one over the cycle-adjusted P/E) less the US 10-year Treasury yield after inflation: what equities offer over bonds in real terms. Lower than its history means equities are priced richly against bonds. Every sleeve score above is relative; this is the absolute check.</p>\n<dl class="gm-takeaways" id="bd-notes" data-source="macro_history scores"></dl>\n<div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-static" id="bd-erp" data-source="macro_history scores"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div>\n<div class="gm-chart" id="bd-chart" data-source="macro_history" style="min-height:280px"></div>\n<h3 class="gm-h3">What has worked in backdrops like today\'s<button type="button" class="gm-i" data-info="regime" aria-label="About the regime table">i</button></h3>\n<p class="gm-note">Each pillar\'s 12-month rank correlation with later returns in months like each backdrop, and the top fifth\'s average 12-month excess. Today\'s states are highlighted.</p>\n<div class="gm-heat" id="bd-regime-heat" data-source="bt_regime macro_history"></div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block" id="worldmap">\n<h2 class="gm-h2">Country composites</h2>\n<p class="gm-note">Each country sleeve\'s composite under the current settings; hover for its cycle-adjusted P/E and currency valuation, and click to open it on the Equity scorecard. Other countries have no sleeve.</p>\n<div class="gm-chart" id="wm-chart" data-source="scores"></div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block" id="portfolio">\n<h2 class="gm-h2">Your portfolio against the signals<button type="button" class="gm-i" data-info="portfolio" aria-label="About the portfolio check">i</button></h2>\n<p class="gm-note">Paste holdings as ticker and weight, one per line or separated by commas, for example <span class="gm-mono">SPY 40, EFA 20, EEM 10, XLK 5</span>. Tickers outside the universe are listed but not scored. The list stays in this page\'s link.</p>\n<div class="gm-pfin"><textarea id="pf-in" rows="3" spellcheck="false" aria-label="Holdings and weights"></textarea><div class="gm-pfbtn"><button type="button" class="gm-btn" id="pf-go">Check portfolio</button><button type="button" class="gm-btn gm-btn2" id="pf-clear">Clear</button></div></div>\n<dl class="gm-takeaways" id="pf-notes" data-source="scores correlations"></dl>\n<div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-static" id="pf-table" data-source="scores"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block">\n<h2 class="gm-h2">Country tilts against ACWI<button type="button" class="gm-i" data-info="tilt" aria-label="About country tilts">i</button></h2>\n<p class="gm-note">Starts from ACWI\'s live country weights and tilts each country by its sleeve\'s score. Countries without a sleeve stay at a neutral score.</p>\n<div class="gm-inputs">\n<label class="gm-field"><span class="gm-label">Maximum relative tilt</span><span class="gm-numin"><input id="a-max" type="number" value="50" min="0" max="100" step="5">%</span></label>\n<label class="gm-field"><span class="gm-label">Neutral band (± points)</span><span class="gm-numin"><input id="a-band" type="number" value="5" min="0" max="25" step="1"></span></label>\n<label class="gm-field"><span class="gm-label">Score basis</span><select id="a-basis"><option value="composite">Composite</option><option value="fundamentals">Fundamentals</option><option value="valuation">Valuation</option><option value="technicals">Technicals</option><option value="sentiment">Sentiment</option></select></label>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-static" id="tilt" data-source="acwi_countries scores"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block">\n<h2 class="gm-h2">Country cross-checks<button type="button" class="gm-i" data-info="alCross" aria-label="About these cross-checks">i</button></h2>\n<p class="gm-note">Beside each country&rsquo;s score: whether its earnings yield covers the return FMP&rsquo;s country risk premiums say investors should require, its region&rsquo;s growth surprises, and the currency model&rsquo;s view. Shown for judgment; not part of the score.</p>\n<div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-compact gm-static" id="al-cross" data-source="country_risk"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block">\n<h2 class="gm-h2">Style, size and region preferences</h2>\n<p class="gm-note">Each pair compares two sleeves on the selected score basis. A gap inside the neutral band reads as neutral.</p>\n<div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-static" id="pairs" data-source="scores"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div>\n</div>',scorecard:'<div class="gm-hrow"><h2 class="gm-h2">Model scorecard<button type="button" class="gm-i" data-info="msTab" aria-label="About the model scorecard">i</button></h2></div>\n<p class="gm-note">Every model on one page, and the allocation models run together. Backtests are gross of trading costs and use only data available at each date; the live record starts with the first saved weekly call.</p>\n<div class="gm-block"><div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-compact gm-static" id="ms-table" data-source="model_sb model_fi model_ra"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div></div>\n<div class="gm-block">\n<h3 class="gm-h3">The allocation models together<button type="button" class="gm-i" data-info="combBt" aria-label="About this backtest">i</button></h3>\n<ul class="gm-bul" id="cb-notes" data-source="model_bt"></ul>\n<div class="gm-grid2">\n<div class="gm-chart" id="cb2-cum" data-source="model_bt" style="min-height:260px"></div>\n<div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-compact gm-static" id="cb2-stats"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block"><h3 class="gm-h3">How the models did in backdrops like today&rsquo;s</h3><div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-compact gm-static" id="ms-reg"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div></div>\n<div class="gm-block"><h3 class="gm-h3">Signal registry<button type="button" class="gm-i" data-info="registry" aria-label="About the registry">i</button></h3>\n<p class="gm-note">Every signal in the allocation models, with its 12-month rank correlation before 2019 and from 2020, and whether it is live. New signals joined a model only if they reached 0.05 to 2018 and did not fall below &minus;0.05 from 2020, a rule fixed before testing.</p>\n<div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-compact gm-static" id="ms-registry" data-source="signal_registry"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div></div>',sbbt:'<div class="gm-hrow"><h2 class="gm-h2">Stocks vs bonds: backtest and live record<button type="button" class="gm-i" data-info="sbTab" aria-label="About this backtest">i</button></h2></div>\n<p class="gm-note">Monthly from June 2016, gross of costs, using only data available at each date. <button type="button" class="gm-linkbtn" data-go="sb">Back to the current call</button></p>\n<div class="gm-grid2">\n<div class="gm-block"><h3 class="gm-h3">Backtest: model against a static 60/40</h3><div class="gm-chart" id="sb-cum" data-source="model_sb" style="min-height:260px"></div></div>\n<div class="gm-block"><h3 class="gm-h3">Statistics</h3><div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-compact gm-static" id="sb-stats"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div></div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-grid2">\n<div class="gm-block"><h3 class="gm-h3">Versions of the model<button type="button" class="gm-i" data-info="sbVar" aria-label="About the versions">i</button></h3><div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-compact gm-static" id="sb-var"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div></div>\n<div class="gm-block"><h3 class="gm-h3">Which signals have worked<button type="button" class="gm-i" data-info="sbIc" aria-label="About this table">i</button></h3><div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-compact gm-static" id="sb-ic"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div></div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-grid2">\n<div class="gm-block"><h3 class="gm-h3">Results by backdrop</h3><div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-compact gm-static" id="sb-reg" data-source="model_sb"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div></div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block"><h3 class="gm-h3">Live record</h3><div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-compact gm-static" id="sb-live" data-source="model_log"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div><p class="gm-note">Each week&rsquo;s saved call, held until the next one: the gain from the equity weight differing from 60%, in ACWI and AGG.</p></div>',fibt:'<div class="gm-hrow"><h2 class="gm-h2">Fixed income relative value: backtest and live record<button type="button" class="gm-i" data-info="frTab" aria-label="About this backtest">i</button></h2></div>\n<p class="gm-note">Monthly from December 2010, gross of costs, using only data available at each date. <button type="button" class="gm-linkbtn" data-go="firv">Back to the current tilts</button></p>\n<div class="gm-grid2">\n<div class="gm-block"><h3 class="gm-h3">Backtest: model against the US Aggregate</h3><div class="gm-chart" id="fr-cum" data-source="model_fi" style="min-height:260px"></div></div>\n<div class="gm-block"><h3 class="gm-h3">Statistics</h3><div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-compact gm-static" id="fr-stats"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div></div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-grid2">\n<div class="gm-block"><h3 class="gm-h3">Results by backdrop</h3><div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-compact gm-static" id="fr-reg" data-source="model_fi"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div></div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block"><h3 class="gm-h3">Live record</h3><div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-compact gm-static" id="fr-live" data-source="model_log"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div><p class="gm-note">Each week&rsquo;s saved tilts, held until the next one, measured with the pair funds.</p></div>',macro:'<div class="gm-hrow"><h2 class="gm-h2">Macro and regime<button type="button" class="gm-i" data-info="macroTab" aria-label="About the macro page">i</button></h2></div>\n<ul class="gm-bul" id="mc-lead" data-source="macro_state"></ul>\n<div class="gm-grid2">\n<div class="gm-block"><h3 class="gm-h3">Where the economy is</h3><div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-compact gm-static" id="mc-axes" data-source="macro_state"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div><p class="gm-note">Each axis averages its inputs, each scaled against its own history to that month, and is shown in standard deviations of its own history. Probability is the chance the axis is on its current side, from a normal curve.</p></div>\n<div class="gm-block"><div class="gm-hrow"><h3 class="gm-h3">Regime map</h3><span class="gm-seg" id="mc-view"><button type="button" data-view="gi" aria-pressed="true">Growth &times; inflation</button><button type="button" data-view="gf">Growth &times; conditions</button><button type="button" data-view="if">Inflation &times; conditions</button><button type="button" data-view="gif">All three</button></span></div>\n<div class="gm-chart" id="mc-map" data-source="macro_state"></div><div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-compact gm-static" id="mc-states" data-source="macro_state"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div></div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block"><h3 class="gm-h3">What drives each axis</h3><div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-compact gm-static" id="mc-inputs" data-source="macro_state"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div></div>\n<div class="gm-block"><div class="gm-hrow"><h3 class="gm-h3">How the backdrop has played out<button type="button" class="gm-i" data-info="playbook" aria-label="About the playbook">i</button></h3><span class="gm-seg" id="mc-pbk"><button type="button" data-pbk="asset" aria-pressed="true">Assets</button><button type="button" data-pbk="sleeve">Equity sleeves</button><button type="button" data-pbk="model">Models</button></span></div>\n<p class="gm-note" id="mc-pb-note"></p><div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-compact gm-static" id="mc-pb" data-source="regime_playbook"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div></div>\n<div class="gm-grid2">\n<div class="gm-block"><div class="gm-hrow"><h3 class="gm-h3">Data against forecasts<button type="button" class="gm-i" data-info="surprise" aria-label="About surprise indices">i</button></h3><span class="gm-seg" id="mc-sk"><button type="button" data-sk="growth" aria-pressed="true">Growth</button><button type="button" data-sk="inflation">Inflation</button></span></div>\n<div class="gm-chart" id="mc-sur" data-source="surprise" style="min-height:280px"></div><div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-compact gm-static" id="mc-surt" data-source="macro_state"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div></div>\n<div class="gm-block"><h3 class="gm-h3">Biggest surprises, past three weeks</h3><div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-compact gm-static" id="mc-recent" data-source="macro_state"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div>\n<h3 class="gm-h3" style="margin-top:18px">Coming up</h3><p class="gm-note" id="mc-up-note"></p><div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-compact gm-static" id="mc-up" data-source="calendar_live"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div></div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block" id="mc-calls-blk">\n<div class="gm-hrow"><h3 class="gm-h3">What companies are saying: S&amp;P 500 earnings calls<button type="button" class="gm-i" data-info="callsTab" aria-label="About the earnings-call readings">i</button></h3><span class="gm-seg" id="mc-callv"><button type="button" data-cv="chg" aria-pressed="true">Tone vs own history</button><button type="button" data-cv="tone">Tone</button><button type="button" data-cv="guide">Net guidance</button></span></div>\n<ul class="gm-bul" id="mc-calls-lead" data-source="call_index"></ul>\n<div class="gm-grid2">\n<div><div class="gm-chart" id="mc-calls-chart" data-source="call_index" style="min-height:240px"></div></div>\n<div><div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-compact gm-static" id="mc-topics" data-source="call_index"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div><p class="gm-note">Share of calls in the past three months that mention each topic, against its own history since 2014.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-grid2">\n<div><h4 class="gm-h4">Tone improved most, largest companies, past 45 days</h4><div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-compact gm-static" id="mc-mv-up" data-source="call_index"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div></div>\n<div><h4 class="gm-h4">Tone deteriorated most</h4><div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-compact gm-static" id="mc-mv-dn" data-source="call_index"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div></div>\n</div>\n<h4 class="gm-h4">Has it worked?</h4><ul class="gm-bul" id="mc-calls-test" data-source="call_tests"></ul>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-grid2">\n<div class="gm-block"><h3 class="gm-h3">Positioning in futures<button type="button" class="gm-i" data-info="positioning" aria-label="About positioning">i</button></h3><p class="gm-note" id="mc-pos-note"></p><div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-compact gm-static" id="mc-pos" data-source="positioning"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div></div>\n<div class="gm-block"><h3 class="gm-h3">Policy rates</h3><div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-compact gm-static" id="mc-pol" data-source="macro_state"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div><p class="gm-note">From the rate decisions in FMP&rsquo;s economic calendar.</p></div>\n</div>',fxm:'<div class="gm-hrow"><h2 class="gm-h2">Currency views<button type="button" class="gm-i" data-info="fxmTab" aria-label="About the currency models">i</button></h2></div>\n<ul class="gm-bul" id="fxm-lead" data-source="fx_model"></ul>\n<div class="gm-block"><div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-compact gm-static" id="fxm-table" data-source="fx_model"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div><p class="gm-note">Scores run from &minus;1 to +1; positive means the currency is expected to gain against the dollar (for the dollar index, the dollar to gain). Combined is 60% cyclical (6 to 12 months) and 40% long-term (3 to 5 years). The suggested hedge for developed currencies moves from 50% toward 0% when the view is positive and toward 100% when it is negative. Click a row to see its history.</p></div>\n<div class="gm-grid2">\n<div class="gm-block"><div class="gm-hrow"><h3 class="gm-h3">Score history</h3><select id="fxm-sel"></select></div><div class="gm-chart" id="fxm-hist" data-source="fx_model" style="min-height:240px"></div></div>\n<div class="gm-block"><h3 class="gm-h3" id="fxm-drv-h">What drives the view</h3><div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-compact gm-static" id="fxm-drv" data-source="fx_model"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div></div>\n</div>\n<p class="gm-note"><button type="button" class="gm-linkbtn" data-go="fxbt">Backtests and which signals have worked</button> &middot; <button type="button" class="gm-linkbtn" data-go="fx">Currency markets</button></p>',lab:'<div class="gm-hrow"><h2 class="gm-h2">Model lab<button type="button" class="gm-i" data-info="labTab" aria-label="About the model lab">i</button></h2></div>\n<p class="gm-note">Re-run the allocation models with your own pillar weights, signals and position sizes, on the same monthly history the published models use: every signal is known only at its own month-end, and returns are gross of costs. Signals marked &ldquo;Evidence only&rdquo; failed the promotion rule and are off in the published models; you can switch them on here.</p>\n<div class="gm-barrow"><span class="gm-label">Model</span><span class="gm-seg" id="lab-model"><button type="button" data-lm="sb" aria-pressed="true">Stocks vs bonds</button><button type="button" data-lm="fi">Fixed income</button><button type="button" data-lm="ra">Real assets</button><button type="button" data-lm="all">All together</button></span><button type="button" class="gm-linkbtn" id="lab-reset">Reset to the published settings</button></div>\n<ul class="gm-bul" id="lab-lead" data-source="lab_inputs"></ul>\n<div class="gm-grid2">\n<div class="gm-block"><h3 class="gm-h3">Experiment against the published settings</h3><div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-compact gm-static" id="lab-stats" data-source="lab_inputs"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div><p class="gm-note">&ldquo;Up to 2018&rdquo; is the period the promotion rule judges signals on; it also requires &ldquo;From 2020&rdquo; not to be clearly negative, so neither period is a clean out-of-sample test. The only true test is the live record from here on.</p><p class="gm-note" id="lab-check"></p><div id="lab-res" data-source="lab_inputs lab_spec"></div></div>\n<div class="gm-block"><h3 class="gm-h3">Growth of 100</h3><div class="gm-chart" id="lab-cum" data-source="lab_inputs" style="min-height:280px"></div></div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-grid2">\n<div class="gm-block"><h3 class="gm-h3">Settings</h3><div id="lab-ctl" class="gm-labctl" data-source="lab_spec"></div></div>\n<div class="gm-block">\n<h3 class="gm-h3">The call today</h3><div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-compact gm-static" id="lab-now" data-source="lab_inputs"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div>\n<div class="gm-labrun"><button type="button" class="gm-btn" id="lab-run" aria-pressed="false">Run the whole dashboard on these settings</button><p class="gm-note">The portfolio, Deploy cash, scenarios, the summary and the headline then use the experiment&rsquo;s current calls until you switch back. Only you see it, and only until you reload.</p></div>\n<h3 class="gm-h3">Saved experiments<button type="button" class="gm-i" data-info="labSaved" aria-label="About saved experiments">i</button></h3>\n<div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-compact gm-static" id="lab-saved" data-source="lab_experiments"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div>\n<div class="gm-labsave"><label class="gm-field"><span class="gm-label">Name this experiment</span><input type="text" id="lab-name" maxlength="80" placeholder="e.g. Trend-led with positioning"></label>\n<textarea id="lab-code" readonly rows="3" aria-label="Experiment settings to copy"></textarea>\n<button type="button" class="gm-btn" id="lab-copy">Copy</button>\n<p class="gm-note">To save an experiment for everyone who opens the dashboard, copy it and send it to Claude in chat or as a comment. Claude adds it to this list, and the weekly refresh keeps a live record for it from then on. Making an experiment the published model is also done through Claude, and the change is logged here with its date.</p></div>\n</div>\n</div>',fxbt:'<div class="gm-hrow"><h2 class="gm-h2">Currencies: backtest<button type="button" class="gm-i" data-info="fxmTab" aria-label="About the currency models">i</button></h2></div>\n<p class="gm-note">Monthly from 2008, gross of costs, using only data available at each month-end. The strategy holds each of 14 currencies long or short against the dollar in proportion to its score, scaled by its volatility, with 100% gross exposure. <button type="button" class="gm-linkbtn" data-go="fxm">Back to the current views</button></p>\n<ul class="gm-bul" id="fxb-notes" data-source="fx_model"></ul>\n<div class="gm-grid2">\n<div class="gm-block"><h3 class="gm-h3">Growth of 100</h3><div class="gm-chart" id="fxb-cum" data-source="fx_model" style="min-height:260px"></div></div>\n<div class="gm-block"><h3 class="gm-h3">Results</h3><div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-compact gm-static" id="fxb-stats" data-source="fx_model"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div></div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-grid2">\n<div class="gm-block"><h3 class="gm-h3">Rank correlation with later returns</h3><div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-compact gm-static" id="fxb-ic" data-source="fx_model"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div><div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-compact gm-static" id="fxb-agg" data-source="fx_model"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div></div>\n<div class="gm-block"><h3 class="gm-h3">Which signals have worked</h3><div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-compact gm-static" id="fxb-comp" data-source="fx_model"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div><p class="gm-note">Cyclical signals against 6-month returns, long-term signals against 36-month returns. &ldquo;Dollar timing&rdquo; applies each signal to the dollar index against its next 12 months.</p></div>\n</div>',history:'<template id="tpl-history">\n<div class="gm-block" id="changes">\n<h2 class="gm-h2">What moved this week<button type="button" class="gm-i" data-info="waterfall" aria-label="About this chart">i</button></h2>\n<p class="gm-note" id="wf-note"></p>\n<div class="gm-chart" id="wf-chart" data-source="score_history" style="min-height:200px"></div>\n<div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-static" id="wf-table" data-source="score_history"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block" id="ledger">\n<h2 class="gm-h2">Call ledger<button type="button" class="gm-i" data-info="ledger" aria-label="About the call ledger">i</button></h2>\n<p class="gm-note">Every sleeve that entered or left the top and bottom fifth at each weekly snapshot, under the current weights and sentiment reading.</p>\n<div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-static" id="lg-table" data-source="score_history"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block" id="track">\n<h2 class="gm-h2">Live track record<button type="button" class="gm-i" data-info="liveTrack" aria-label="About the live track record">i</button></h2>\n<p class="gm-note">Out of sample: every weekly snapshot\'s top and bottom fifth under the current weights and sentiment reading, held until the next snapshot and chained, against all sleeves equally weighted. Prices are dividend-adjusted closes. This is the only test the model cannot have been fitted to.</p>\n<dl class="gm-takeaways" id="tr-notes" data-source="score_history etf_history"></dl>\n<div class="gm-chart" id="tr-chart" data-source="score_history etf_history" style="min-height:260px"></div>\n<div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-static" id="tr-table" data-source="score_history etf_history"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block">\n<h2 class="gm-h2">Live scores against a saved snapshot<button type="button" class="gm-i" data-info="history" aria-label="About score history">i</button></h2>\n<div class="gm-inputs">\n<label class="gm-field"><span class="gm-label">Compare with</span><select id="h-date" data-source="score_history"></select></label>\n<label class="gm-field"><span class="gm-label">Improving or fading at ± points</span><span class="gm-numin"><input id="h-band" type="number" value="5" min="1" max="30" step="1"></span></label>\n</div>\n<dl class="gm-takeaways" id="h-notes" data-source="scores score_history"></dl>\n<div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-static" id="hist" data-source="scores score_history"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div>\n<p class="gm-note">A snapshot is saved each time Claude rebuilds the fundamentals. Ask Claude to save one at any time, or to do it on a weekly schedule.</p>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block">\n<h2 class="gm-h2">Reconstructed score history<button type="button" class="gm-i" data-info="recon" aria-label="About reconstructed history">i</button></h2>\n<p class="gm-note">The model rebuilt month by month since 2011 from what was knowable at each date: prices, quarterly financials and holdings breadth. It has no growth or analyst inputs, so it is close to, but not the same as, today\'s live scores.</p>\n<div class="gm-inputs">\n<label class="gm-field"><span class="gm-label">Sleeve 1</span><select id="hh-1"></select></label>\n<label class="gm-field"><span class="gm-label">Sleeve 2</span><select id="hh-2"></select></label>\n<label class="gm-field"><span class="gm-label">Sleeve 3</span><select id="hh-3"></select></label>\n<label class="gm-field"><span class="gm-label">Measure</span><select id="hh-m"><option value="composite">Composite</option><option value="rank">Rank in universe</option><option value="quality">Quality</option><option value="valuation">Valuation</option><option value="technicals">Technicals</option><option value="sentiment">Sentiment</option></select></label>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-chart" id="hh-chart" data-source="sleeve_series" style="min-height:300px"></div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block">\n<h2 class="gm-h2">Composite by quarter</h2>\n<p class="gm-note">Reconstructed composite at each quarter-end. Green is a high score, red a low one.</p>\n<div class="gm-tablewrap"><div class="gm-heat gm-qheat" id="hh-heat" data-source="sleeve_series"></div></div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block">\n<h2 class="gm-h2">Snapshot log</h2>\n<div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-static" id="snap-log" data-source="score_history"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div>\n</div>\n</template>',backtest:'<div class="gm-barrow gm-btwin"><span class="gm-label">History<button type="button" class="gm-i" data-info="btWin" aria-label="About the two histories">i</button></span><span class="gm-seg" id="bt-win"><button type="button" data-bw="full" aria-pressed="true">Full, from 2011</button><button type="button" data-bw="filed">Filed holdings only</button></span><span class="gm-note" id="bt-win-note"></span></div>\n<template id="tpl-backtest">\n<div class="gm-block">\n<h2 class="gm-h2">Backtest of the equity model, 2011 to today<button type="button" class="gm-i" data-info="btTab" aria-label="About the backtest">i</button></h2>\n<p class="gm-note">The model rebuilt at every month-end since 2011 from data that was available at the time, including each fund\'s holdings as filed with the SEC, then held for the following month. Valuation is the cycle-adjusted P/E against each sleeve\'s own history, as in the live model; it starts in late 2019, and before that composites use the other three pillars. Read the caveats at the bottom before drawing conclusions.</p>\n<dl class="gm-takeaways" id="bt-notes" data-source="bt_stats bt_ic bt_valuation"></dl>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block" id="honesty">\n<h2 class="gm-h2">How much to trust these results<button type="button" class="gm-i" data-info="honesty" aria-label="About these checks">i</button></h2>\n<dl class="gm-takeaways" id="ho-notes" data-source="bt_honesty"></dl>\n<h3 class="gm-h3">Excess return over all sleeves, with a 90% range</h3>\n<div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-static gm-hm" id="ho-strat" data-source="bt_honesty"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div>\n<h3 class="gm-h3">Rank correlation with later returns, with a 90% range</h3>\n<div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-static gm-hm" id="ho-ic" data-source="bt_honesty"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block">\n<h2 class="gm-h2">Growth of 100</h2>\n<div class="gm-chips" id="bt-chips"></div>\n<label class="gm-check"><input type="checkbox" id="bt-log"> Log scale</label>\n<div class="gm-chart" id="bt-chart" data-source="bt_performance" style="min-height:340px"></div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block">\n<h2 class="gm-h2">Strategy statistics</h2>\n<div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-static gm-hm" id="bt-stats" data-source="bt_stats"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block">\n<h2 class="gm-h2">Does a higher score predict higher returns?<button type="button" class="gm-i" data-info="ic" aria-label="About the information coefficient">i</button></h2>\n<p class="gm-note">Average rank correlation between each score and the sleeves\' subsequent returns, by holding horizon. Above zero means higher scores were followed by higher returns.</p>\n<div class="gm-chart" id="bt-ic" data-source="bt_ic"></div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block" id="calib">\n<h2 class="gm-h2">Does a higher score mean a better next year?</h2>\n<p class="gm-note" id="cb-note"></p>\n<div class="gm-chart" id="cb-chart" data-source="bt_settings"></div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block">\n<h2 class="gm-h2">Every setting, tested<button type="button" class="gm-i" data-info="btSettings" aria-label="About the settings table">i</button></h2>\n<p class="gm-note" id="bts-note"></p>\n<div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-static gm-hm" id="bt-settings" data-source="bt_settings"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div>\n<p class="gm-note" id="bts-custom"></p>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block">\n<h2 class="gm-h2">Which way of measuring valuation held up?<button type="button" class="gm-i" data-info="valBasis" aria-label="About how valuation is measured">i</button></h2>\n<p class="gm-note">Rank correlation between each valuation score and subsequent returns at month-ends from 2017; the cycle-adjusted read has values only from late 2019, so its figures cover a shorter, more recent window. The 12-month figure is also split by region. Above zero means cheaper sleeves went on to do better.</p>\n<div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-static gm-hm" id="bt-val" data-source="bt_valuation"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block">\n<h2 class="gm-h2">Does the result depend on the holdings used?</h2>\n<p class="gm-note">The same tests with holdings as filed at the time against today\'s holdings applied to the past, over the full period and over the window in which every sleeve has filed holdings. Rank correlation with returns over the following 12 and 36 months, and the top fifth\'s average 12-month return over the universe average.</p>\n<div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-static gm-hm" id="bt-rob" data-source="bt_robustness"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block">\n<h2 class="gm-h2">Returns by score fifth</h2>\n<div class="gm-inputs">\n<label class="gm-field"><span class="gm-label">Score</span><select id="bq-s"><option value="composite">Composite</option><option value="fundamentals">Fundamentals (quality)</option><option value="valuation">Valuation, cycle-adjusted own history</option><option value="valuation_grp">Valuation, within category</option><option value="valuation_own">Valuation, all multiples vs own history</option><option value="valuation_xs">Valuation, across the universe</option><option value="pct_capePE">Cycle-adjusted P/E alone</option><option value="pct_peTTM">Trailing P/E alone</option><option value="technicals">Technicals</option><option value="sentiment">Sentiment</option><option value="cmp_evidence_confirm">Backtest-informed, confirming</option></select></label>\n<label class="gm-field"><span class="gm-label">Horizon</span><select id="bq-h"><option value="1">1 month</option><option value="3">3 months</option><option value="6">6 months</option><option value="12" selected>12 months</option><option value="24">24 months</option><option value="36">36 months</option></select></label>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-chart" id="bt-q" data-source="bt_quintiles"></div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block">\n<h2 class="gm-h2">Rolling three-year excess return over ACWI</h2>\n<div class="gm-chart" id="bt-roll" data-source="bt_rolling" style="min-height:280px"></div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block">\n<h2 class="gm-h2">Weight presets compared</h2>\n<div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-static gm-hm" id="bt-presets" data-source="bt_presets"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block gm-prose">\n<h3>Caveats</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Holdings as filed, with a gap at the start.</b> Each month uses the latest holdings report the fund had filed with the SEC (form N-PORT) by that date, including companies that have since left the fund. FMP\'s filings start between late 2019 and late 2021 depending on the fund; before a fund\'s first filing, its earliest filing stands in, so the early years still carry some look-ahead. The robustness table shows the results over the fully filed window too.</li>\n<li><b>No growth or estimate inputs.</b> FMP has no history of consensus estimates or targets, so the backtested fundamentals pillar is quality only, and sentiment uses fund flows only. Monthly analyst rating history does exist from 2019; rating drift was tested and did not help at this horizon.</li>\n<li><b>The backtest-informed preset is fitted to this sample.</b> Its weights were chosen after seeing these results, so its backtest flatters it. It is a sensible starting point, not proof.</li>\n<li><b>A single regime.</b> 2011 to 2026 was dominated by US large-cap growth. Cheap sleeves (international, EM, value) mostly kept getting cheaper, which is why valuation detracted. A fifteen-year sample is short for a 12 to 36 month signal.</li>\n<li><b>Frictionless.</b> All backtests on this dashboard are gross: no trading costs, taxes or cash drag. Monthly rebalancing of the top fifth turns over roughly a third of the portfolio a month.</li>\n<li><b>Point in time where possible.</b> Quarterly financials are used 60 days after the quarter ends; prices, moving averages and breadth use only data up to each month-end.</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</template>',method:'<div class="gm-method">\n<nav class="gm-toc" aria-label="Methodology sections">\n<span class="x-tocg">Start here</span>\n<a href="#m-purpose">Purpose</a>\n<a href="#m-fresh">What updates when</a>\n<a href="#m-read">How to use it</a>\n<span class="x-tocg">Allocation models</span>\n<a href="#m-dec">Decision summary</a>\n<a href="#m-sb">Stocks vs bonds model</a>\n<a href="#m-firv">Fixed income model</a>\n<a href="#m-ra">Real assets &amp; alternatives</a>\n<a href="#m-pm">Private markets</a>\n<a href="#m-fx">Currencies</a>\n<a href="#m-mp">Model portfolio and risk</a>\n<a href="#m-live">Live records and alerts</a>\n<a href="#m-macro">Macro, regimes, positioning</a>\n<span class="x-tocg">Markets, companies, commentary</span>\n<a href="#m-short">Short term and what&rsquo;s changing</a>\n<a href="#m-fixed">Bond markets</a>\n<a href="#m-credit">Inflation, real yields and credit</a>\n<a href="#m-xlab">Compare &amp; correlate</a>\n<a href="#m-cos">Companies</a>\n<a href="#m-prose">How the commentary is written</a>\n<span class="x-tocg">Equity scorecard</span>\n<a href="#m-pillars">The four pillars</a>\n<a href="#m-sentiment">How sentiment is read</a>\n<a href="#m-lookthrough">Fund-level numbers</a>\n<a href="#m-scoring">Scoring</a>\n<a href="#m-weights">Weights and signals</a>\n<a href="#m-deploy">Deployment rules</a>\n<a href="#m-tools">Visuals, trends, allocation</a>\n<a href="#m-history">Score history</a>\n<a href="#m-breadth">Breadth and concentration</a>\n<a href="#m-returns">Returns and time series</a>\n<a href="#m-backtest">Equity backtest</a>\n<a href="#m-metrics">Metric reference</a>\n<a href="#m-universe">Universe and coverage</a>\n<span class="x-tocg">Data</span>\n<a href="#m-data">Data and freshness</a>\n<a href="#m-dq">Data checks</a>\n<a href="#m-limits">Limitations</a>\n<a href="#m-changes">What changed</a>\n</nav>\n<div class="gm-prose">\n<h3 id="m-purpose">Purpose</h3>\n<div class="x-intent" role="note"><p><strong>This is not a systematic investment strategy.</strong> The dashboard exists to synthesize the weight of the evidence — valuation, fundamentals, trend, sentiment, macro, earnings calls and the record of each signal — so that a person can make better-informed decisions. It is intentionally flexible: every model, weight and threshold can be changed, and no output is meant to be followed mechanically.</p><p><strong>It is built to support investor judgment, not to replace it.</strong> Read the scores, model calls and commentary as inputs to a human decision-making process, alongside context the data cannot capture.</p></div>\n<p>The dashboard covers a global multi-asset portfolio: how much to hold in equities against bonds, how to tilt the bond portfolio against the US Aggregate, which diversifiers and currencies are in a supportive state, and, inside equities, which regions, styles, sectors and countries look better or worse supported. The decisions are framed over 12 to 36 months; the Today page and the daily commentary cover what moves in between.</p>\n<p>The equity part is a relative-opportunity screen for the building blocks of a global equity allocation, from core indexes down to sectors, industries and single countries. It asks one question: over the next 12 to 36 months, which parts of the equity opportunity set look better or worse supported than the rest? It ranks; it does not forecast returns. Use it to decide where to spend research time and to challenge existing tilts, not as a trading signal.</p>\n<p>Every position, in every asset class, is held through a liquid US-listed ETF. Equity fundamentals come from looking through each ETF to the companies it holds; bond, real-asset and currency views come from prices, yields, distributions and the macro record. Prices are live.</p>\n<h3 id="m-fresh">What updates when</h3>\n<p>The page combines layers of data that refresh on different clocks. Opening it during the day gives a current read on prices and everything built from them; the models and the fundamental view behind the rankings move once a week; the commentary is written on its own schedule.</p>\n<div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-static gm-nosortt gm-fresht">\n<thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>What it covers</th><th>Refreshed</th><th>Current as of</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><b>Live</b></td><td>ETF quotes and daily price history; quotes for every holding in the look-through; Treasury yields; dollar, credit, oil, gold and VIX proxies. Everything computed from them: technicals, short-term returns, What&rsquo;s changing, breadth, the price-driven repricing of valuation multiples, target upside, and every score, rank and composite.</td><td>Each time the page opens</td><td id="mf-live">—</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Weekly</b></td><td>The allocation models (stocks vs bonds, fixed income, real assets &amp; alternatives, private markets, currencies) and the saved review behind the live records and the decision summary; the macro record, surprises and futures positioning; FRED breakevens, real yields and credit spreads; fund holdings and weights; company fundamentals, consensus estimates and revisions; analyst ratings and targets; fund flows and fees; earnings-call scores; company profiles; the dated score snapshot; the weekly note, its saved doc and the committee pack.</td><td>Saturday 6:45 am Pacific, by a scheduled task</td><td id="mf-snap">—</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Monthly</b></td><td>The backtest, the rebuilt monthly history of every sleeve since 2010, regime results, calibration and the horizon-fitted weights.</td><td>First Saturday of each month</td><td id="mf-bt">—</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Commentary</b></td><td>The Day Ahead and Day in Review on trading days; Week in Review, The Week Ahead, Month in Review and Quarter in Review; earnings-call takeaways in busy reporting weeks. Each ends with the models&rsquo; live track record.</td><td>Trading days 5:30 am and 3 pm PT;<br>Fri after the close; Sun afternoon</td><td>—</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><b>What a daily look tells you.</b> Where markets are now, and how price moves since Saturday have changed the equity ranks: technicals and repriced valuations move every day, so a sleeve that sells off gets cheaper on the page the same day. Fundamentals, estimates and holdings behind it are as of the weekly snapshot.</li>\n<li><b>What changes only on Saturday.</b> The model calls, the macro record, earnings, margins, growth, estimate revisions, analyst ratings, flows and the note. If a company reports midweek, the page reflects it after the next rebuild.</li>\n<li><b>Sources.</b> Market, company, economic-calendar and positioning data come from FMP. Breakevens, TIPS real yields and ICE BofA credit spreads and returns by rating come from FRED. Purchasing-power parity factors are the World Bank&rsquo;s. Holdings history for the equity backtest comes from SEC filings through FMP. Headlines on the Today page come from major financial outlets.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id="m-read">How to use it</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Today</b> is the daily read: market moves, headlines from major financial outlets, and the economic and earnings calendar with filters.</li>\n<li><b>Executive summary</b> is the decision page. The decision summary at the top gives each view and its size, what changed since the previous saved review, the observations behind it, how strong the evidence is, what dominates the resulting portfolio and what would reverse each call. Below it: the combined model portfolio and its risk, what markets have done over the period you pick, what is changing, the risks to the view and how much to trust each model.</li>\n<li><b>Commentary</b> holds the written pieces, from The Day Ahead to the Quarter in Review.</li>\n<li><b>Decide</b> holds the forward-looking models. Allocation: stocks vs bonds, fixed income relative value, real assets &amp; alternatives (with private markets through listed proxies), currencies and the model lab. Equities: the scorecard that ranks 62 equity sleeves, relative trends (each dimension&rsquo;s score against its relative price), country and style allocation with the portfolio check, cash deployment and scenarios.</li>\n<li><b>Markets</b> looks back: multi-asset performance across 36 assets from one day to twenty years; the macro backdrop; Compare &amp; correlate for any assets across classes; equity pulse, short-term returns, long-run returns, time series, visuals and breadth; the Treasury curve and bond markets; inflation, real yields and credit; currencies and the pair explorer.</li>\n<li><b>Companies</b> holds the earnings-call analysis and the company profiles for the largest holdings.</li>\n<li><b>Evidence</b> holds the model scorecard, each model&rsquo;s backtest, the equity live record and score history, and this page.</li>\n<li>The universe filter and equity model settings appear only on the equity pages, where they apply. Click any table heading to sort; ranked charts have their own sort control; every time-series chart has a time-frame control.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id="m-dec">Decision summary</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>What it shows.</b> One row per view (stocks vs bonds, duration, bond sectors, equity regions, equity style and size, diversifiers, private markets, the dollar): the tilt and its size, the evidence grade, the main observation behind it and the first condition that would reverse it. Opening a row shows every driver, the context the models do not use, what supports and weakens the call, the reversal levels and the coverage warnings.</li>\n<li><b>Reversal levels.</b> For stocks vs bonds they are solved from the live score&rsquo;s own arithmetic: the level of one input, others held where they are, at which the equity weight would go to neutral or to the mirror-image underweight. For bond levers they are the yield gaps at which a position starts and reaches full size.</li>\n<li><b>Evidence grade.</b> A fixed rubric, applied the same way to every view: +1 if every live signal predicted in both halves of the record (before 2019 and from 2020), &minus;1 if one has turned negative since 2020; +1 if a live signal has a t-stat of 1.5 or more since 2020; +1 if the backtest&rsquo;s 90% range is above zero, &minus;1 if the timing itself lost money; +1 if the live inputs agree, &minus;1 if they offset. Strong is 3 or more, Moderate 2, Mixed 1, Weak 0 or less.</li>\n<li><b>What changed.</b> Each weekly review saves its calls and the observations behind them. The page compares the latest review with the previous saved one using those saved values; recorded market series such as Treasury yields are read at the earlier review&rsquo;s date. When there is no earlier review, the page says so instead of reconstructing one from backtest history.</li>\n<li><b>What dominates the portfolio.</b> The equity/bond split against the reference, expected volatility and tracking error, the share of active risk by decision, exposure to equities, rates and the dollar from the risk model, the largest funds and look-through companies, and the current stock&ndash;bond correlation.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id="m-sb">Stocks vs bonds model</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>What it decides.</b> The equity weight in a portfolio of global equities (ACWI) and US bonds (AGG), around a 60/40 neutral and within 45% to 75%: 60% plus 15 points times the blended score.</li>\n<li><b>Four pillars, weighted 50/0/25/25.</b> Valuation 50%, momentum 0%, macro 25%, risk 25%. A signal carries weight only if it passes the promotion rule (see Macro, regimes, positioning); a pillar with no passing signal drops out and the others are reweighted.</li>\n<li><b>Live now.</b> Valuation: the bond term premium, read from the 2s10s slope against its history (a steep curve pays bond holders more, so it counts against equities). Macro: global growth surprises. These two carry the whole score; the risk pillar has no passing signal.</li>\n<li><b>Shown, not used.</b> The equity risk premium (the point-in-time S&amp;P 500 cycle-adjusted earnings yield, built from the index&rsquo;s members and earnings as filed, less the 10-year Treasury yield minus trailing CPI inflation); high yield against duration-matched Treasuries over six months; the US growth nowcast; three-month equity volatility against its three-year median; S&amp;P 500 futures positioning; and momentum, equities against bonds over 12 months and against their 10-month average. Each failed the promotion rule and is displayed as context.</li>\n<li><b>Real yield.</b> The models use one definition everywhere: the 10-year Treasury yield less trailing CPI inflation, lagged a month. The market&rsquo;s TIPS real yield is shown separately on the inflation and credit page and on the decision summary.</li>\n<li><b>Scoring.</b> Each input is a z-score against its own history up to that month, clipped at two standard deviations and squashed to &minus;1 to +1. Nothing uses data that was not available at the time.</li>\n<li><b>Backtest.</b> Monthly from June 2016, when global growth surprises begin: the weight set at each month-end earns the next month&rsquo;s ACWI and AGG returns. Compared with a static 60/40, with a block-bootstrap 90% range, months ahead, turnover and each signal&rsquo;s rank correlation with the following one and twelve months. The excess is split into the average tilt and the timing around it.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id="m-firv">Fixed income relative value model</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>What it decides.</b> Tilts against the US Aggregate that keep risk close to the benchmark&rsquo;s: hold AGG and apply duration-aware swaps between bond ETFs.</li>\n<li><b>Duration</b> (&plusmn;1.5 years, long Treasuries against short): longer when real yields are high and the curve is steep against their history.</li>\n<li><b>Investment-grade credit</b> (&plusmn;15%, LQD against IEF) and <b>agency MBS</b> (&plusmn;10%, MBB against IEF): their spread signals did not pass the promotion rule, so both are held at the Aggregate&rsquo;s weight until one does.</li>\n<li><b>High yield</b> (up to 10%, HYG against IEI) and <b>EM dollar debt</b> (up to 8%, EMB against IEF): added only when cheap, since the Agg holds none.</li>\n<li><b>TIPS</b> (up to 10%, TIP against IEI): when inflation has been rising over six months.</li>\n<li><b>EM local currency</b> (up to 5%, EMLC against short Treasuries): after a strong six-month dollar run, which leaves EM currencies cheap. Local yields and the currency model are shown as evidence only.</li>\n<li><b>International bonds, hedged</b> (&plusmn;10%, BNDX against IEF): toward hedged foreign bonds when the hedged yield pickup is high against its history, that is when foreign yield curves (10-year less 3-month) are steeper than the US curve. Foreign yields are OECD monthly averages from FRED, used a month late. The six-month change in the US-minus-foreign yield gap is shown as evidence only. Because hedged foreign bonds barely move with US yields, this swap lowers the portfolio&rsquo;s US-rate exposure.</li>\n<li><b>Signals</b> follow the same z-score method and promotion rule as the stocks vs bonds model. Spreads are distribution yields over Treasury ETFs of similar duration, a proxy for option-adjusted spreads, which FMP does not carry.</li>\n<li><b>Backtest.</b> Monthly from December 2010, when at least five levers have enough history: the Agg plus each lever&rsquo;s position times the return of its long leg less its short leg. Reported against the Agg: excess return with a 90% range, tracking error, information ratio, months ahead, worst run behind, and each lever&rsquo;s contribution, hit rate and rank correlation.</li>\n<li><b>Design choice.</b> An earlier version also used momentum and risk-off (VIX) signals. Over this period they reduced returns at the 12-month horizon, so the model now buys value only. That choice was made after seeing results; treat the backtest as an upper bound.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id="m-mp">Model portfolio and risk</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Combination.</b> Equities get the policy neutral plus the band times the stocks vs bonds score; the equity share is first split into country funds by tilting ACWI&rsquo;s country weights with the equity scores (the Equity allocation page settings), with countries that have no fund held through ACWX. Then up to the sleeve budget (20% of equities by default, at most 5% in any one sleeve) moves into the best-scoring sleeves anywhere in the equity universe: sectors, industries, US and international styles and sizes, factors and regional funds. A sleeve qualifies when its composite is more than the allocation band (at least 5 points) above 50; weights follow how far above, up to ten sleeves, skipping any that moves almost one-for-one (correlation above 0.92) with a better-scoring pick. Each is funded pro rata from the broad and country funds of its own region, so region weights, and the region look-through, are unchanged. Negative views are expressed by not holding a sleeve; nothing is sold short. Bonds are the Agg plus the fixed income pair trades. An optional diversifier sleeve, funded pro rata, holds the real assets that score above 0.1, weighted by score over volatility with at most 35% in any one.</li>\n<li><b>Reference.</b> The neutral weight in ACWI, the rest in AGG. The table shows ACWI split into the same country funds, so active weights read country by country.</li>\n<li><b>Risk.</b> A covariance matrix of every fund the portfolio can hold (all 62 equity sleeves plus the bond, real-asset, alternative and dollar funds) from the last five years of monthly returns. The active risk is split into the stocks vs bonds call, the country tilts, the sleeve tilts, holding country funds instead of ACWI (an implementation choice), the fixed income tilts and the diversifiers. Tracking error is the volatility of the active weights; each decision&rsquo;s contribution is its covariance with the total active position divided by the tracking error, so contributions add to the total.</li>\n<li><b>Scenarios.</b> Each fund&rsquo;s sensitivity to global equities, the 10-year yield, the dollar and oil comes from a five-year monthly regression; a shock is applied through those sensitivities to every fund and to the portfolio and reference.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id="m-ra">Real assets and commodities</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Assets.</b> Gold (GLD), broad commodities (DBC), US REITs (VNQ), global infrastructure (IGF), managed futures (DBMF), copper (CPER), energy pipelines (AMLP) and Bitcoin, judged as diversifiers for a 60/40 portfolio. Proxies fill early history: a trend-following construction for managed futures before mid-2019 (ten markets, long or short on the sign of the 12-month return, equal risk, less a 0.85% fee), and the front copper future before CPER in late 2011. Bitcoin enters in 2015.</li>\n<li><b>Signals.</b> Every asset has trend (the 12-month return and the price against its 10-month average) and diversification (the three-year correlation with 60/40, good below 0.5, kept for all as a risk property, not a forecast). Each also has its own candidates: real yields for gold; the inflation nowcast and oil and copper positioning for commodities; the REIT yield over the 10-year; trend strength across the markets it trades and financial stress for managed futures; global growth, China growth surprises and positioning for copper; the pipeline yield over the 10-year and oil positioning for MLPs; financial conditions and the dollar model for Bitcoin. Each faces the promotion rule every week; the page and the signal registry show which are live. Each signal is scaled to &minus;1 to +1 and the live ones are averaged.</li>\n<li><b>Use in the portfolio.</b> The diversifier sleeve on the model portfolio is 0% unless you set it; when used, it holds the assets that score above 0.1, weighted by score over three-year volatility (so a volatile asset such as Bitcoin does not swamp the sleeve&rsquo;s risk), with at most 35% in any one.</li>\n<li><b>Evidence.</b> The 12-month rank correlation of the score with the next year&rsquo;s return over 60/40 since 2009 is small for every asset; read the scores as a guide to which diversifier is in a supportive state, not as a forecast.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id="m-pm">Private markets, through listed proxies</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>What it decides.</b> Relative-value calls, not diversifiers: business development companies (BIZD) against high yield (HYG), and listed private equity (PSP) against global equities (ACWI). The calls are shown on Real assets &amp; alternatives and Relative trends; they are not held in the model portfolio.</li>\n<li><b>Signals.</b> For BDCs: the discount to book value (the median price to last reported book value of twelve BDCs, each book value counted 60 days after its quarter ends), the BDC distribution yield over high yield, and the 12-month relative trend. For listed private equity: the relative trend, the high yield spread against its history and financial conditions. Each faces the promotion rule; a pair with no live signal makes no call.</li>\n<li><b>Caveat.</b> Listed proxies trade daily and move more than the private funds they stand in for, and their history starts in 2006 (PSP) and 2013 (BIZD).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id="m-fx">Currencies</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Coverage.</b> Six developed and six emerging currencies against the dollar, quoted as dollars per unit so a rise means the currency gained. Weekly history back to 2005; daily returns are live.</li>\n<li><b>Valuation.</b> Against the currency&rsquo;s own 10-year average, and against the World Bank&rsquo;s purchasing-power-parity rate (positive means cheap). PPP gaps are large and persistent for emerging currencies; compare them with their own history, not with zero.</li>\n<li><b>Hedge ratio.</b> For dollar-based holders of developed-market equities, from the currency model: 50%, moving toward 0% when it expects the currency to gain and toward 100% when it expects it to fall. Where the model has no view, a rule: 50%, plus 25 points if the currency is more than 5% above its 10-year average, minus 25 if more than 5% below, plus 25 if it fell more than 2% over 12 months, minus 25 if it rose more than 2%, within 0% to 100%. A rule of thumb, not a backtested model.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id="m-live">Live records, alerts and the committee pack</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Live records.</b> Each Saturday the stocks vs bonds weight, the fixed income tilts and their pair funds, the real asset scores, the currency views, the regime, the equity preferences and the observations behind them are saved. Each call is held until the next one and measured with daily fund prices; the Model scorecard page compounds them.</li>\n<li><b>Running count.</b> Every commentary piece ends with the live track record to its data date: stocks vs bonds against a 60/40, fixed income tilts against the Aggregate, and the favored minus the avoided equity sleeves, since the first saved call and for the latest session. A record this short says little about skill; it is shown so the count is honest from the start.</li>\n<li><b>Alerts.</b> The scheduled rebuild emails a short note when a call changes materially: the equity weight by 3 points or more, a fixed income tilt changing sign or moving 5 points (half a year for duration), a real asset score crossing zero, or a change in the top or bottom equity sleeves.</li>\n<li><b>Committee pack.</b> A PDF of the weekly note, the model calls and the main charts, rebuilt each Saturday and linked from the Executive summary.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id="m-short">Short term and what&rsquo;s changing</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Periods.</b> 1D is today&rsquo;s change against the prior close. WTD runs from the last close before Monday. 1W is five trading days, MTD from the last close of the prior month, 1M is 21 trading days. All are total returns from dividend-adjusted prices ending at the live price. QTD runs from the last close of the prior quarter, 3M and 6M are 63 and 126 trading days, 1Y is 252. 2Y comes from daily prices; 3Y to 20Y from weekly prices since 2005. From 2Y on, returns are annualized, and funds without the full history are left out of that period. &ldquo;vs global equities&rdquo; shows each return relative to ACWI.</li>\n<li><b>Unusual moves</b> are measured in standard deviations of the sleeve&rsquo;s own daily returns over the past three months, scaled to the period. Beyond two is flagged.</li>\n<li><b>What&rsquo;s changing</b> is rule-based. It flags style, size and region pairs whose one-month relative return reverses their 12-month direction; 12-month leaders that lose ground and laggards that rebound; crosses of the 200-day average; new 52-week highs and lows; outsized weekly moves; shifts in breadth, return dispersion and volatility; rates, dollar, credit, oil and gold moving against their six-month trend; sleeves the data favors that the market is selling and the reverse; and regime labels that changed over three months. Items are ordered by how far they sit from normal.</li>\n<li>Short-term returns are context, not inputs. The ranking uses 12-month momentum and trend, not the last week.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id="m-prose">How the commentary is written</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Commentary tab.</b> Written by Claude scheduled tasks from FMP and FRED data, in a house voice: The Day Ahead before each trading day&rsquo;s open, Day in Review after its close, Week in Review on Fridays, The Week Ahead on Sundays, Month and Quarter in Review after each period&rsquo;s last close, and earnings-call takeaways in busy reporting weeks. Every number is checked against the data pulled before publishing; figures that cannot be confirmed are cut. The weekly, monthly and quarterly pieces are also emailed.</li>\n<li><b>Page text.</b> Every other sentence on the dashboard (the headline, the decision summary, the scorecard, category notes, What&rsquo;s changing and the weekly note) is generated from the numbers, by rule, each time the page opens. Nothing is edited by hand.</li>\n<li>For each equity sleeve, a metric is cited as support when it sits in the most favorable quarter of the universe, and as a weakness when it sits in the least favorable quarter. Cycle-adjusted P/E is judged against the sleeve&rsquo;s own history instead, using the top and bottom 30% of its range. The strongest two or three are quoted with their values.</li>\n<li>Category notes name the top and bottom sleeves under the current settings, the pillar that most separates them from the rest of the category, the forward P/E range and recent returns. A line marked Against the view flags recent performance that contradicts the ranking.</li>\n<li>Sentiment evidence follows the sentiment setting: read contrarian, outflows and low Buy shares count in a sleeve&rsquo;s favor and crowded positioning against it.</li>\n<li>Critical points are in bold.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id="m-fixed">Bond markets</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Coverage.</b> 27 bond ETFs: Treasuries by maturity and TIPS; the US aggregate; agency MBS, AAA CLOs and municipals; investment-grade corporates by rating and maturity, and floating-rate notes; BB and broad high yield, fallen angels and senior loans; EM dollar and local-currency debt and hedged international bonds; preferreds and convertibles.</li>\n<li><b>Curve.</b> FMP Treasury yields, live, from one month to 30 years, compared with one day, one week, one month, three months and one year earlier. The curve is described as bull or bear (yields falling or rising) and steepening or flattening, from the 2-year and 10-year moves. Month-end history since 2005 comes from the weekly rebuild.</li>\n<li><b>Returns.</b> Dividend-adjusted total returns to the live price, over the same periods as every other asset; 3 to 20 years are annualized from daily history since 2005. Returns against duration-matched Treasuries subtract the return of Treasury ETFs interpolated to the same empirical duration.</li>\n<li><b>Yield.</b> Trailing 12-month distributions over today&rsquo;s price, and the latest distribution annualized. These are distribution yields, not yields to maturity: they lag market yields as coupons reset, they include TIPS inflation accruals, and municipal yields are before tax.</li>\n<li><b>Duration and equity beta.</b> Weekly returns over three years are regressed on the weekly change in the Treasury yield at the maturity that fits best and on US equity returns. The yield coefficient is an empirical duration; the equity coefficient shows how much credit behaves like stocks. Floating-rate and loan funds show near-zero duration by construction.</li>\n<li><b>Yield over Treasuries.</b> A fund&rsquo;s trailing yield less the trailing yield of Treasury ETFs interpolated to the same duration. Using distribution yields on both sides cancels most of the coupon lag. It is a spread proxy, not an option-adjusted spread.</li>\n<li>Holdings-level bond data (ratings mix, maturities) is not available from FMP, so fund-level credit measures come from prices and distributions. Index option-adjusted spreads by rating come from FRED, on the inflation, real yields and credit page.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id="m-credit">Inflation, real yields and credit</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Inflation and real yields.</b> Treasury breakevens (5-year, 10-year and the 5-year, 5-year forward) and TIPS real yields (5, 10 and 30 years) from FRED, daily since 2003, with the 10-year yield split into its real yield and breakeven.</li>\n<li><b>Credit.</b> ICE BofA option-adjusted spreads and total-return indexes for investment grade and high yield, and by rating from AAA to CCC, from FRED. FRED carries only the last three years of these series, so spread history and returns by rating start about three years back.</li>\n<li>Rebuilt with each full price refresh and every Saturday.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id="m-xlab">Compare &amp; correlate</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Coverage.</b> Equity sleeves, bond funds, real assets, commodity futures, alternatives (managed futures, BDCs, listed private equity) and uranium miners, the dollar index and twelve currencies, bitcoin and ether, the VIX, and Treasury yields, real yields, breakevens and credit spreads, in one list.</li>\n<li><b>History.</b> Weekly closes from 2005&ndash;07 for the non-equity assets, joined to the daily history of the last few years and scaled where they meet. Rates and spreads are levels in percent from FRED.</li>\n<li><b>Views.</b> Growth of 100, relative to a benchmark, drawdown, rolling volatility, rolling correlation and levels; statistics, a correlation matrix (daily, weekly or monthly returns) and calendar-year returns follow the time frame you pick. Yields and spreads are compared in basis-point changes, not returns.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id="m-cos">Companies</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Earnings calls.</b> Every S&amp;P 500 earnings call since 2014, scored for tone, guidance and topics; seasons are calendar quarters with at least 25 calls, and changes compare each company with its own call a year earlier. Tone readings roll up to sectors and sleeves and are tested against later returns like any other signal.</li>\n<li><b>Company profile.</b> The largest holdings across the dashboard&rsquo;s funds (about 300), with price history against the market and the sector, revenue and earnings with estimates, margins, cash flow, the balance sheet, the earnings record, key metrics, call tone, which funds hold it, and peers. In the Claude version any ticker can be looked up live.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class="x-mgroup">Equity scorecard</p>\n<p class="gm-note">The sections from here to the metric reference describe the equity scorecard, which ranks the 62 equity sleeves and feeds the equity country tilts in the model portfolio.</p>\n<h3 id="m-pillars">The four pillars</h3>\n<p>Each pillar answers a different question, and they are chosen so that some pull against each other. A sleeve that scores well on all four is rare; most of the insight comes from where they disagree.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Fundamentals</b>, made of quality and growth, asks whether the underlying businesses are good and getting better. Quality covers profitability, cash backing of earnings, leverage and how predictable earnings are. Growth covers consensus earnings and revenue growth over the current and next fiscal year, how broad that growth is across holdings, and, once 13 weeks of weekly snapshots exist, how consensus estimates have been revised.</li>\n<li><b>Valuation</b> asks what you are paying for those fundamentals. It is the slowest-acting pillar: a weak guide to the next few months but one of the better guides to multi-year returns. It deliberately pulls against fundamentals, because the best businesses are rarely the cheapest.</li>\n<li><b>Technicals</b> asks whether the market agrees. It rewards a steady, intact uptrend, because medium-term momentum tends to persist and because a trend filter keeps the screen from favoring markets that are cheap because they are still falling.</li>\n<li><b>Sentiment</b> asks how crowded the trade is, from analyst ratings and targets and from fund flows. By default it is read contrarian, so it pulls against technicals: a strong trend that everyone already owns scores lower than one the crowd has not yet joined. The next section explains why.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id="m-sentiment">How sentiment is read</h3>\n<p>Sentiment measures positioning: how much optimism is already in the price. It has three inputs: how many analysts rate the holdings a buy, how far their price targets sit above today\'s prices, and how much money has flowed into the fund over six months (growth in the ETF\'s shares outstanding). Price breadth and RSI used to sit here too. Read contrarian, they pointed the wrong way in the backtest, because they are really momentum measures, so they now appear only on the Breadth tab. The test results are in the table below.</p>\n<div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-static gm-hm" id="m-cand" data-source="bt_candidates"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div>\n<p><b>Contrarian (default).</b> Over 12 to 36 months, extremes in positioning tend to reverse. When nearly every analyst rates a market a buy and most of its stocks are at highs, good news is priced in, expectations are hard to beat and there are few marginal buyers left. When sentiment is washed out, the opposite holds. In this mode, crowded optimism lowers the score and pessimism raises it.</p>\n<p><b>Confirming.</b> Every sentiment metric flips, so optimism and broad participation raise the score. This suits shorter horizons or a regime where you expect trends to keep running. In this mode sentiment largely repeats what technicals already measures, so the composite leans harder toward momentum. Because scores are percentile ranks, a sleeve\'s confirming sentiment score is exactly 100 minus its contrarian score. Each mode has its own composite and group rank, and the switch in the equity settings changes every equity view on the page.</p>\n<p>Reading technicals and contrarian sentiment together gives four setups:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Strong trend, washed-out sentiment:</b> a recovery the crowd has not joined yet. Usually the most attractive combination.</li>\n<li><b>Strong trend, crowded sentiment:</b> a mature trend. It can keep going, but expect less upside and sharper pullbacks.</li>\n<li><b>Weak trend, washed-out sentiment:</b> capitulation. A contrarian opportunity if fundamentals and valuation support it, though it usually pays to wait for the trend to turn.</li>\n<li><b>Weak trend, crowded sentiment:</b> optimism the market is not confirming. The weakest setup.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Two metrics that used to sit in sentiment now live in fundamentals, because they describe earnings rather than positioning. Analyst agreement on this year\'s EPS is part of quality, as earnings visibility. The share of holdings with rising next-year EPS is part of growth, as growth breadth.</p>\n<h3 id="m-lookthrough">How fund-level numbers are built</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Holdings.</b> Each ETF is looked through to its largest holdings, enough to cover most of its weight. Concentrated funds are covered almost completely. Broad, equal-weight and small-cap funds hold hundreds or thousands of names, so coverage is lower; each sleeve\'s coverage is shown under its holdings table and in the universe table below. Weights are re-scaled across the holdings that have data for each metric.</li>\n<li><b>Valuation multiples</b> are aggregated the way index providers do it: the weighted average of each company\'s earnings, book, EBITDA or cash-flow yield is inverted back into a multiple. This keeps a handful of very expensive or loss-making names from dominating the result.</li>\n<li><b>Growth</b> is earnings-weighted: it is the growth of the fund\'s aggregate earnings, not an average of company growth rates, so a small company tripling its earnings from a low base does not swamp the result. Revenue growth is weighted the same way, by sales.</li>\n<li><b>Forward P/E</b> uses a next-twelve-months EPS that blends the current and next fiscal year by the time left in the current one. Estimates are in each company\'s reporting currency, so forward multiples are scaled from FMP\'s trailing P/E, which keeps currencies consistent. Companies whose estimates don\'t reconcile with their trailing EPS are left out of growth and forward P/E.</li>\n<li><b>Quality</b> uses weighted medians, which are robust to outliers. Banks and insurers are excluded from cash conversion, return on invested capital, net debt to EBITDA, EV to EBITDA and free cash flow yield, where their accounts do not compare with other companies.</li>\n<li><b>Coverage rule.</b> A fund-level metric is shown only when holdings worth at least a quarter of the fund have usable data. Otherwise it is left blank and the pillar is averaged from the metrics that remain.</li>\n<li><b>Live repricing.</b> Fundamentals are a snapshot, but valuation multiples move with each ETF\'s price since the snapshot date, so a sell-off makes a sleeve cheaper on the page the same day.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id="m-scoring">How scores are built</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Percentile ranks.</b> Each metric is ranked across the whole universe and expressed as a percentile from 0 to 100, oriented so that higher is always better under the current reading. Ranks rather than raw values keep one extreme number from dominating and put metrics on a common scale.</li>\n<li><b>Pillars.</b> A pillar is the simple average of its metrics\' percentiles. Fundamentals averages quality and growth.</li>\n<li><b>Composite.</b> The equal-weight average of fundamentals, valuation, technicals and sentiment.</li>\n<li><b>Valuation: cycle-adjusted P/E against the sleeve\'s own history.</b> By default the valuation pillar asks one question: is this sleeve\'s price high or low relative to its average earnings over the last five years, compared with its own record since 2011? Each sleeve\'s position in its own range is then ranked across the universe. This is the logic of Shiller\'s CAPE: it ignores sector and country mix, which dominate any comparison of one sleeve\'s multiple with another\'s, and it does not mistake peak-cycle earnings for cheapness.</li>\n<li><b>Why this default.</b> Five ways of measuring valuation were tested. Every method that compares multiples across sleeves, or uses ordinary trailing multiples, pointed the wrong way in a decade led by US growth. The cycle-adjusted own-history read was the only one with a positive record (12-month rank correlation +0.08, top fifth ahead of the bottom fifth by about 6% over the next year), in the US and in developed markets abroad. It needs three years of earnings and two years of its own record, so its test runs only from late 2019 and is not statistically strong. The Valuation control switches to the other four: within category, all multiples against own history, across the universe, or a blend.</li>\n<li><b>Still relative.</b> The final score is always a rank, so if everything is expensive, something still scores 100.</li>\n<li><b>Cycle-adjusted P/E.</b> Valuation includes price over the sleeve\'s average trailing earnings of the last five years, from its rebuilt monthly history. Ordinary P/Es look cheapest at the top of an earnings cycle; the five-year average largely removes that trap.</li>\n<li><b>Conviction.</b> Every sleeve is also ranked under all 70 combinations of the seven presets, two sentiment readings and five valuation bases. Conviction is how often it lands in the top or bottom fifth. The Bottom line on the Scoreboard lists the sleeves that do so in at least 60% of settings, flags leaders whose margins sit at the top of their own range or whose data failed a check, and states what the current setting has meant historically.</li>\n<li><b>Group rank.</b> Narrow industries and broad indexes compete in the same ranking. The In group column, and the universe filter, let you compare like with like.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id="m-changes">What changed in this version</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>October 2026.</b> A Today page; a decision summary at the top of the Executive summary; a Commentary tab with daily, weekly, monthly and quarterly pieces that each carry the live track record; Companies (earnings calls and company profiles); inflation, real yields and credit from FRED; relative trends for every equity dimension; Compare &amp; correlate across asset classes and a currency pair explorer; a time-frame control on every time-series chart; the promotion rule applied to every signal, original ones included; a public copy of the dashboard that refreshes prices every five minutes in market hours.</li>\n<li><b>One portfolio.</b> A combined model portfolio with adjustable policy settings and a risk budget by decision; live records and a scorecard for every model; triggers that would change each call and results by backdrop; a real assets model; a currencies page; cross-asset scenarios; email alerts and a weekly committee pack. Trading costs are no longer modeled anywhere: all backtests are gross. The equity risk premium and real yield now have one definition each across the dashboard.</li>\n<li><b>Multi-asset tool.</b> An executive summary up front; two allocation models (stocks vs bonds, and fixed income relative value against the US Aggregate) with their own backtests; multi-asset and fixed income market pages; navigation regrouped into Decide, Markets and Evidence.</li>\n<li><b>Navigation.</b> Sections replaced the original thirteen tabs, with the pulse page (then called Overview) first. Model settings fold away under the universe filter. Every table sorts by its headings and ranked charts have their own sort.</li>\n<li><b>Short term and what&rsquo;s changing.</b> Returns from one day to one month, live rates, dollar, credit and commodity moves, and a rule-based list of moves that run against the longer-term picture.</li>\n<li><b>Commentary from the data.</b> The headline, the scorecard summary, the category notes and the weekly note now quote the underlying numbers rather than the scores. The weekly note is in short bullets.</li>\n<li><b>Longer, truer history.</b> The backtest now starts in 2011 rather than 2015, and the cycle-adjusted P/E uses earnings restated for inflation. On the longer record the cycle-adjusted own-history valuation read is still the best of the five tested, but only slightly positive (12-month rank correlation about +0.03), so valuation is a weak tilt rather than a strong one.</li>\n<li><b>Consistent forward P/E.</b> Forward P/E is price over next-twelve-month consensus EPS with both in the company\'s reporting currency (prices quoted in pence, cents or agorot are converted first). It no longer mixes adjusted estimates with reported trailing earnings. Where too little of a fund has a usable figure, the old scaled method stands in.</li>\n<li><b>New metrics.</b> Net share issuance counts in quality (dilution is bad). Shareholder yield (dividends plus net buybacks) and currency valuation count in the within-category, cross-universe and blended valuation reads. Margin trend, currency trend, fees and fund flows are shown alongside; flows now drive sentiment.</li>\n<li><b>Turnover buffer and smoothing.</b> The backtest compares the monthly top fifth with a buffered version (enter in the top fifth, leave only below the top 40%) and with a three-month average score. The buffer keeps most of the return at about a third of the trading.</li>\n<li><b>Separate bets.</b> Sleeves are clustered by return correlation, so the Bottom line and Deploy tab say how many independent bets a list of preferences really is.</li>\n<li><b>Horizon-fitted weights.</b> Three presets weight the pillars by how well each predicted returns at 6, 12 and 36 months, shrunk halfway to equal and floored at zero. They are fitted to the backtest, so their own backtest flatters them.</li>\n<li><b>Expected return and confidence.</b> Each sleeve\'s score is translated into an expected 12-month return against the universe with a 90% range, and graded A to D for how much independent evidence supports the call.</li>\n<li><b>Within-category test.</b> Picking the top fifth inside each category, which neutralizes region and style bets, still beat the universe in the backtest, so the edge is not only a region or style call.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id="m-weights">Weight presets and signals</h3>\n<p>The composite blends the four pillars with one of seven preset weightings, or your own. Each preset is a different investment stance:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Equal weight</b> (25% each): no stance. The default.</li>\n<li><b>Value tilt</b> (fundamentals 30, valuation 40, technicals 15, sentiment 15): leans on starting valuation, the slowest but most persistent driver of multi-year returns.</li>\n<li><b>Momentum</b> (15, 15, 50, 20): follows the trend. Suits shorter holding periods.</li>\n<li><b>Quality</b> (45, 25, 15, 15): favors durable, growing businesses at a reasonable price.</li>\n<li><b>Contrarian</b> (20, 35, 10, 35): buys cheap, unloved markets. Pairs naturally with the contrarian sentiment reading.</li>\n<li><b>Long horizon, 12 to 36 months</b> (30, 35, 20, 15): the blend most consistent with this tool\'s horizon.</li>\n<li><b>Fitted to 6, 12 or 36 months:</b> weights from each pillar\'s rank correlation with returns at that horizon, shrunk halfway to equal; see the Backtest tab for the current figures.</li>\n<li><b>Backtest-informed</b> (50, 10, 20, 20): leans on quality, the one pillar with a consistently positive record in the backtest, and keeps only a small valuation weight. It held up under both sentiment readings and both holdings bases, but its weights were chosen after seeing the results, so treat its backtest as flattering.</li>\n<li><b>Custom:</b> enter any four weights; they are rescaled to sum to 100%.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>When a sleeve is missing a pillar, its composite is the weighted average of the pillars it has. Signals follow the composite: at or above the Overweight threshold reads Overweight, at or below the Underweight threshold reads Underweight, and anything between is Neutral. Both thresholds are adjustable above the tabs.</p>\n<h3 id="m-deploy">Deployment rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Sleeves are ranked by composite under the current weights, sentiment reading and universe filter.</li>\n<li>A sleeve is eligible when its composite clears the minimum and, if required, it has both a fundamentals and a valuation score. Global blends (ACWI, ACWX) can be excluded, since they overlap with everything else.</li>\n<li>Picks are taken in rank order, skipping any sleeve whose 36-month return correlation with an earlier pick is above the maximum. This keeps the picks from being one bet three times.</li>\n<li>The amount is split in proportion to how far each pick\'s composite sits above 50, so stronger conviction gets more money.</li>\n<li>The avoid list is the lowest composites in the current universe. None of this is advice; it is a disciplined way to turn the screen into a short list.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id="m-tools">Visuals, trends and allocation</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Four-box matrix:</b> any two pillars against each other, split at the universe midpoint of 50, with the members of each box listed.</li>\n<li><b>Metric explorer:</b> any two metrics or pillars across the sleeves, with a least-squares line, R² and correlation. The strongest-relationships table ranks every pair of metrics by R², optionally only pairs from different pillars, which shows which signals are telling you the same thing.</li>\n<li><b>Relative strength:</b> a sleeve\'s month-end price divided by a benchmark\'s, rebased to 100. A rising line means it is outperforming.</li>\n<li><b>Correlation:</b> correlation of monthly dividend-adjusted returns over the latest 36 months. Below about 0.5 is a meaningful diversifier.</li>\n<li><b>Equities against bonds:</b> the cycle-adjusted earnings yield of the US, global, developed ex-US and emerging sleeves less the US real 10-year yield (10-year Treasury less trailing CPI inflation), with its range since 2011. The regime table labels each month by rates, curve, dollar, credit, inflation and equity trend, and shows how each pillar ranked sleeves in months like each state.</li>\n<li><b>Portfolio check:</b> paste holdings and weights to see the portfolio\'s weighted scores, the share of weight in each signal, holdings ranked low under most settings, sleeves you could add that are in the top fifth under most settings and least correlated with what you hold, and signal-tilted weights using the tilt settings below.</li>\n<li><b>Country tilts:</b> each country\'s ACWI weight is scaled by one plus the maximum tilt times its score\'s distance from 50 (as a share of 50), then all weights are rescaled to sum to 100%. Scores inside the neutral band count as 50. Countries without a sleeve stay neutral.</li>\n<li><b>Pair preferences:</b> the score gap between two sleeves; a gap inside the neutral band reads as neutral.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id="m-history">Score history</h3>\n<p>Each time the fundamentals snapshot is rebuilt, every sleeve\'s pillar scores and composites are saved with the date. The History tab compares today\'s live scores with any saved snapshot, under the same weights and sentiment reading, and labels each sleeve improving, fading or stable using the band you set. Live scores move every day with prices; the fundamentals underneath change only when the snapshot is rebuilt.</p>\n<h3 id="m-breadth">Breadth and concentration</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Breadth</b> uses live quotes on every holding in each fund\'s look-through: the weighted share above its own 50- and 200-day averages, within 5% of its 52-week high or low, and up or down today. Narrow breadth under a rising price means a handful of large names is carrying the fund.</li>\n<li><b>Concentration</b> reports the largest holding, the top-10 weight and the effective number of holdings (one over the sum of squared weights, with holdings beyond the look-through assumed equally weighted), plus the largest sector and country and the effective number of sectors.</li>\n<li><b>Sector exposure</b> is each fund\'s look-through sector mix, as a share of the holdings covered.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id="m-returns">Returns and time series</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Historical returns</b> come from dividend-adjusted weekly closes since 2005, ending at today\'s live price. Annualized figures need the full window of history; younger funds show a dash.</li>\n<li><b>Calendar-year and monthly returns</b> use the last weekly close of each year or month.</li>\n<li><b>The time series explorer</b> shows monthly look-through multiples (trailing P/E, price to book, EV to EBITDA, free cash flow and dividend yields), quality metrics, breadth and rebuilt scores for each sleeve since 2011; the median of each across a group or region; and quarterly multiples for each fund\'s 25 largest holdings. Multiples are built from quarterly financials, summed over four quarters and used 60 days after each quarter ends, divided by the price at each month-end.</li>\n<li><b>Against its own history</b>, a single series is shown with its average and a band of one standard deviation, and the table gives today\'s percentile within its own range. This answers the question the cross-sectional scores cannot: is a sleeve cheap compared with its own past?</li>\n<li>Each month uses the holdings the fund had filed by then, so the history describes the fund as it actually was. Before a fund\'s first filing in FMP (late 2019 to late 2021), its earliest filing stands in.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id="m-backtest">Equity backtest</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>How much to trust it.</b> Confidence ranges come from a block bootstrap that resamples whole 12-month stretches of history, so serial correlation is respected. The selection check compares the default setting with all 70 tested: the median setting is the fairer forecast, because the default was chosen after seeing the results.</li>\n<li><b>Live track record.</b> Every weekly snapshot\'s top and bottom fifth is held until the next snapshot, on the History tab. It is the only evidence the model could not have been tuned to, and it needs a year or more before it means much.</li>\n<li>At every month-end from January 2011 the model is rebuilt using only data available then: each fund\'s holdings as filed with the SEC, technicals from prices, quality and valuation from quarterly financials lagged 60 days, and sentiment from six-month fund flows lagged three months. Valuation is the cycle-adjusted P/E against each sleeve\'s own history, as in the live model. That read needs five years of earnings history (at least three) and two years of its own record, so it begins in late 2019; before then, composites are built from the other three pillars. Each pillar is percentile-ranked across the sleeves that existed at that date.</li>\n<li>Growth and estimate inputs cannot be rebuilt (FMP keeps no history of consensus estimates or targets), so the backtested fundamentals pillar is quality only and sentiment uses flows alone. Analyst rating history from 2019 was used to test rating drift, which did not help at 12 to 36 months.</li>\n<li>Three valuation methods are compared side by side, and every test is repeated with today\'s holdings and over the fully filed window, so you can see how much each result depends on those choices.</li>\n<li>Portfolios are held for the following month and rebalanced monthly, equally weighted, with no costs. The 12-month-hold variant averages the last twelve monthly cohorts, matching the model\'s horizon. The deployment rule applies the same picks logic as the Deploy tab.</li>\n<li>The rank correlation between each score and subsequent returns at horizons from one to 36 months shows whether the signal has pointed the right way, and over what horizon. Returns by score fifth show whether the relationship is steady from the bottom to the top of the ranking.</li>\n<li>Treat the results as fifteen years of evidence, dominated by US large-cap growth. A signal that failed in this regime can still be sound, and one that worked can still be luck.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id="m-metrics">Metric reference</h3>\n<p>Every metric, what it measures and how it is scored. The same explanations appear behind the <span class="gm-i gm-i-static" aria-hidden="true">i</span> buttons across the dashboard.</p>\n<div id="m-metric-tables"></div>\n<h3 id="m-universe">Universe and coverage</h3>\n<p>The ETF behind each sleeve, the index it tracks, and how much of the fund the look-through covers.</p>\n<div class="gm-tablewrap">\n<table class="gm-table gm-static" id="m-utable" data-source="etf_universe" data-row-key="etf">\n<thead><tr>\n<th data-field="label">Sleeve</th>\n<th data-field="group">Group</th>\n<th data-field="benchmark" class="gm-hide-sm">Index</th>\n<th data-field="namesUsed">Holdings used</th>\n<th data-field="holdingsCount" class="gm-hide-sm">Total holdings</th>\n<th data-field="weightUsed">Weight covered</th>\n</tr></thead>\n<tbody></tbody>\n</table>\n</div>\n<h3 id="m-data">Data and freshness</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>See <a href="#m-fresh">What updates when</a> at the top of this page for the three refresh layers and their dates.</li>\n<li><b>Proxies:</b> FMP\'s holdings file for IWN was malformed, so the Russell 2000 Value look-through uses Vanguard\'s VTWV, which tracks the same index. IWN\'s own prices still drive its technicals.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id="m-dq">Data checks<button type="button" class="gm-i" data-info="dq" aria-label="About data checks">i</button></h3>\n<p>Checks run on every rebuild and, for quotes, live on every open. Warnings mark numbers to look at before acting on a score; notes are known, expected gaps.</p>\n<div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-static" id="dq-table" data-source="dq_report"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left">Sleeve</th><th style="text-align:left">Check</th><th style="text-align:left">Level</th><th style="text-align:left">Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id="m-macro">Macro record, regimes, positioning and currencies</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>One record of the backdrop.</b> Rebuilt every Saturday and read by every model, narrative and backtest table. It replaces the separate backdrop labels used before.</li>\n<li><b>Surprise indices.</b> For each release in FMP&rsquo;s economic calendar with a consensus forecast (about 170,000 since 2014), the surprise is the actual less the forecast, scaled by that release&rsquo;s own past surprises and signed so good news is positive (unemployment and jobless claims reversed). Releases are sorted into growth and inflation, and averaged by region with a 30-day half-life over 120 days. Global is a GDP-weighted blend.</li>\n<li><b>Three axes.</b> Growth: industrial production, payrolls, unemployment, jobless claims, real retail sales, housing starts, consumer sentiment, and US and global growth surprises. Inflation: CPI acceleration and trend, commodities, import prices, wages, consumer inflation expectations, and inflation surprises. Financial conditions: high yield against Treasuries, the VIX, the dollar, the equity trend, mortgage rates and the 10-year yield. Each input is scaled against its own history to that month; monthly data are lagged a month for publication.</li>\n<li><b>Regimes.</b> Each axis is on one side or the other, with a probability from a normal curve; two axes give four states and three give eight. History runs from 2001.</li>\n<li><b>Positioning.</b> Speculators&rsquo; net position in 23 futures markets as a share of open interest, against its own past three years, from the CFTC reports in FMP. Read as contrarian.</li>\n<li><b>Currency models.</b> Cyclical: the policy-rate gap and its six-month change, the growth-surprise gap, the 12-1 month trend, positioning (contrarian) and sensitivity to financial conditions (havens gain as conditions tighten). Long-term: the real exchange rate against its 10-year average and the real policy-rate gap. Added in October 2026 under the promotion rule (cross-currency 12-month rank correlation, spot plus carry): the six-month change in the 10-year government yield gap against the US (live), and, as candidates, the growth-momentum gap (activity data against the US), the inflation-surprise gap and the inflation gap, which carry weight only once they pass. Purchasing-power parity is shown but left out of the score, since its factors are estimated after the fact; the pair explorer draws both the value model&rsquo;s fair value and PPP. Equal weights within each, as specified before testing. The backtest is also run rebalancing every three and six months.</li>\n<li><b>Promotion rule.</b> Every signal, the original ones included, carries weight only if its 12-month rank correlation with what it predicts is at least 0.05 on signals dated to 2018 and no worse than &minus;0.05 from 2020. Signals that fail stay visible as evidence. Correlations on overlapping 12-month windows overstate the evidence, so each comes with a t-stat that counts only one independent observation a year; few are above 2. Equity valuation uses the point-in-time S&amp;P 500 equity risk premium, built from the index&rsquo;s members on each date and earnings as filed. The registry below lists every signal and its status.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-compact gm-static" id="m-registry" data-source="signal_registry"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id="m-limits">Limitations</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>FMP carries analyst ratings and price targets mainly for US-listed companies, so for international sleeves, sentiment rests mostly on fund flows.</li>\n<li>Small-cap and equal-weight look-throughs cover a minority of each fund, so their fundamentals describe the larger holdings more than the whole fund.</li>\n<li>Consensus estimates can be stale or thin for smaller and emerging-market companies. Memory makers dominate Korea and a large part of EM and Taiwan; their consensus EPS is rising very quickly, which makes those sleeves look cheap on forward P/E and fast-growing.</li>\n<li>Equity scores are cross-sectional: they rank sleeves against each other and say nothing about whether equities as a whole are attractive. That question belongs to the stocks vs bonds model.</li>\n<li>The allocation models rest on few live signals (two for stocks vs bonds), with t-stats mostly below 2 and backtests that start in 2010&ndash;16. Treat their calls as one input, sized modestly.</li>\n<li>Bond spreads in the fixed income model are distribution-yield proxies, not option-adjusted spreads, and FRED&rsquo;s ICE BofA history covers only three years.</li>\n<li>Sector mix drives many fundamental metrics, such as margins, P/B and leverage. Comparing within a group is often more informative than across the whole universe.</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>',xlab:'<template id="tpl-xlab">\n<div class="gm-block">\n<h2 class="gm-h2">Compare &amp; correlate, across asset classes</h2>\n<ul class="gm-bul" id="xl-notes" data-source="etf_history bond_history multi_history fx_history today_px rates_credit"></ul>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block">\n<div class="x-search"><input id="xl-search" list="xl-list" placeholder="Add anything: SPY, gold, 10-year yield, yen, high-yield spread…" aria-label="Add an asset" autocomplete="off"><datalist id="xl-list"></datalist>\n<label class="gm-field"><span class="gm-label">Benchmark</span><select class="x-select" id="xl-bench"></select></label>\n<button type="button" class="gm-linkbtn" id="xl-clear">Clear all</button></div>\n<div class="x-chips" id="xl-picked" style="margin-top:8px"></div>\n<div class="x-ctl"><span class="gm-label">Presets</span><span class="gm-seg gm-presets" id="xl-presets"></span></div>\n<div class="x-ctl"><span class="gm-label">Browse</span><span class="gm-seg" id="xl-cls"></span></div>\n<div class="x-chips" id="xl-quick"></div>\n<p class="gm-note" id="xl-quick-note"></p>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block">\n<div class="gm-hrow"><span class="gm-seg gm-presets" id="xl-mode"></span><span class="gm-seg" id="xl-range"></span></div>\n<div class="x-ctl"><label class="x-check" id="xl-log-wrap"><input type="checkbox" id="xl-log"> Log scale</label></div>\n<div class="gm-chart" id="xl-chart" data-source="etf_history etf_weekly bond_history multi_history fx_history today_px xasset_weekly rates_credit" style="min-height:340px"></div>\n<div class="gm-chart" id="xl-chart2" hidden></div>\n<p class="gm-note" id="xl-chart-note"></p>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block">\n<div class="gm-hrow"><h3 class="gm-h3">Risk and return in this window</h3><span class="gm-seg" id="xl-freq" title="Data frequency for volatility and correlation"></span></div>\n<div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-compact" id="xl-stats" data-source="etf_history bond_history multi_history fx_history today_px rates_credit"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div>\n<p class="gm-note" id="xl-stats-note"></p>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-grid2">\n<div class="gm-block"><h3 class="gm-h3">Correlation matrix</h3><div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-compact x-corr" id="xl-corr" data-source="etf_history bond_history multi_history fx_history today_px rates_credit"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div><p class="gm-note" id="xl-corr-note"></p></div>\n<div class="gm-block"><h3 class="gm-h3">Calendar-year returns</h3><div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-compact" id="xl-cal" data-source="etf_weekly xasset_weekly"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div></div>\n</div>\n</template>',reltrend:'<template id="tpl-reltrend">\n<div class="gm-block">\n<div class="gm-hrow"><h2 class="gm-h2">Relative trends: the model against the market</h2></div>\n<div class="gm-seg gm-presets" id="rt-dim"></div>\n<p class="gm-note" id="rt-dim-note"></p>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-grid2">\n<div class="gm-block">\n<div class="gm-hrow"><h3 class="gm-h3" id="rt-focus-h">—</h3><span class="gm-seg x-range" id="rt-range" aria-label="Time frame"></span></div>\n<div class="gm-chart" id="rt-focus" data-source="sleeve_series score_history etf_weekly" style="min-height:420px"></div>\n<p class="gm-note">Top: the model&rsquo;s reading. Bottom: the price of the favored side relative to the other, rebased to 100 at the start of the time frame.</p>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block">\n<div class="gm-hrow"><h3 class="gm-h3">How predictive has it been?</h3><span id="rt-verdict" class="x-dchip"></span></div>\n<div class="gm-hrow x-rt-hz"><span class="gm-label">Forward</span><span class="gm-seg" id="rt-fwd" aria-label="Forward horizon"></span><span class="gm-label">Sample</span><span class="gm-seg" id="rt-sample" aria-label="Sample period for the statistics"></span></div>\n<ul class="gm-bul" id="rt-facts" data-source="sleeve_series etf_weekly"></ul>\n<h4 class="x-dh4" id="rt-bk-h">Next 12 months, by fifth of the reading</h4>\n<div class="gm-chart" id="rt-buckets" style="min-height:0"></div>\n<p class="gm-note">Each bar is the average relative return over the following forward horizon after months in that fifth of the reading&rsquo;s own history; the label is how often it won. The outlined bar holds today&rsquo;s reading. A model with an edge shows bars rising from left to right.</p>\n<h4 class="x-dh4" id="rt-sc-h">Every month: reading against what followed</h4>\n<div class="gm-chart" id="rt-scatter" style="min-height:0"></div>\n<p class="gm-note">Dark dots are the last three years. The dashed line is the fitted slope; the orange line marks today&rsquo;s reading.</p>\n<h4 class="x-dh4">R² against forward relative returns</h4>\n<div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-compact gm-static x-r2t" id="rt-r2" data-source="sleeve_series etf_weekly"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div>\n<p class="gm-note">How much of the variation in the next 6 to 36 months&rsquo; relative return a straight line through each predictor explains. + means higher values were followed by better relative returns (for trailing returns, the trend persisted); &minus; means they reversed. Click a trailing row to plot it below. All figures use the sample chosen above. Longer horizons overlap heavily, which flatters R².</p>\n<h4 class="x-dh4" id="rt-sc2-h">Trailing relative return against what followed</h4>\n<div class="gm-chart" id="rt-scatter2" style="min-height:0"></div>\n<p class="gm-note" id="rt-sc2-note"></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block">\n<h3 class="gm-h3">Every call in this dimension</h3>\n<p class="gm-note">Top line: the reading; bottom line: relative price. Click a card to enlarge it.</p>\n<div class="x-cards" id="rt-grid" data-source="sleeve_series score_history etf_weekly etf_universe"></div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block">\n<h3 class="gm-h3">Readings, relative returns and how predictive each has been</h3>\n<div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-compact" id="rt-table" data-source="sleeve_series score_history etf_weekly"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div>\n</div>\n</template>',credit:'<template id="tpl-credit">\n<div class="gm-block">\n<div class="gm-hrow"><h2 class="gm-h2">Inflation, real yields and credit</h2><span class="gm-note" id="cr-asof" data-source="rates_credit"></span></div>\n<ul class="gm-bul" id="cr-notes" data-source="rates_credit"></ul>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block">\n<div class="gm-hrow"><h3 class="gm-h3">Inflation breakevens and real yields</h3><span class="gm-seg" id="cr-range"></span></div>\n<div class="gm-grid2">\n<div><h4 class="gm-h4">Breakeven inflation</h4><div class="gm-chart" id="cr-be" data-source="rates_credit"></div></div>\n<div><h4 class="gm-h4">TIPS real yields</h4><div class="gm-chart" id="cr-ry" data-source="rates_credit"></div></div>\n</div>\n<h4 class="gm-h4">The 10-year Treasury: real yield plus breakeven inflation</h4>\n<div class="gm-chart" id="cr-dec" data-source="rates_credit"></div>\n<div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-compact" id="cr-rates" data-source="rates_credit"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div>\n<p class="gm-note">Changes in basis points. Breakevens are the gap between nominal Treasury and TIPS yields: the inflation the market is paying for, plus a risk premium.</p>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block">\n<div class="gm-hrow"><h3 class="gm-h3">Credit spreads by rating</h3><span class="gm-seg" id="cr-oas-seg"></span></div>\n<div class="gm-chart" id="cr-oas" data-source="rates_credit"></div>\n<div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-compact" id="cr-spreads" data-source="rates_credit"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div>\n<p class="gm-note">ICE BofA option-adjusted spreads over Treasuries. FRED publishes the past three years of these series, so ranges and percentiles cover that window. Narrowing spreads show green.</p>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block">\n<div class="gm-hrow"><h3 class="gm-h3">Returns by credit quality</h3><span class="gm-seg" id="cr-ret-per"></span></div>\n<div class="gm-chart" id="cr-ret" data-source="rates_credit"></div>\n<div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-compact" id="cr-returns" data-source="rates_credit"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div>\n<p class="gm-note">Total returns of the ICE BofA US corporate (investment grade) and US high yield indexes by rating, in dollars. Volatility is annualized from weekly returns over the past year.</p>\n</div>\n</template>',calls:'<template id="tpl-calls">\n<div class="gm-block">\n<div class="gm-hrow"><h2 class="gm-h2">Earnings calls: what management is telling us</h2><span class="gm-note" id="cl-season" data-source="call_scores"></span></div>\n<ul class="gm-bul" id="cl-notes" data-source="call_scores call_meta"></ul>\n<p class="gm-note">Every S&amp;P 500 earnings call since 2014, scored by word lists: tone is positive minus negative words over their total, for the whole call and for the Q&amp;A; uncertainty is uncertain words per 1,000; topics count mentions. Index readings use the membership on each call’s date.</p>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block">\n<h3 class="gm-h3">The week’s most important calls, read by Claude</h3>\n<div id="cl-said" data-source="commentary"></div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block">\n<div class="gm-hrow"><h3 class="gm-h3">S&amp;P 500 calls by quarter</h3><span class="gm-seg" id="cl-metric"></span></div>\n<div class="gm-chart" id="cl-trend" data-source="call_scores"></div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block">\n<h3 class="gm-h3">What companies are talking about</h3>\n<div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-compact" id="cl-heat" data-source="call_scores"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block">\n<h3 class="gm-h3">By sector, this season</h3>\n<div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-compact" id="cl-sectors" data-source="call_scores call_meta"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div>\n<p class="gm-note">Change is each company’s tone against its own call a year earlier, averaged; click a sector to filter the companies below.</p>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block">\n<div class="gm-hrow"><h3 class="gm-h3">Companies</h3>\n<label class="gm-field"><span class="gm-label">Sector</span><select id="cl-sector"></select></label></div>\n<div class="gm-seg gm-presets" id="cl-mover"></div>\n<div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-compact" id="cl-movers" data-source="call_scores call_meta"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div>\n<p class="gm-note" id="cl-movers-note"></p>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block">\n<h3 class="gm-h3">Call tone by sleeve</h3>\n<div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-compact" id="cl-sleeves" data-source="call_sleeves"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block">\n<h3 class="gm-h3">Has call tone predicted anything?</h3>\n<div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-compact" id="cl-tests" data-source="call_tests"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div>\n</div>\n</template>',profile:'<template id="tpl-profile">\n<div class="gm-block">\n<div class="x-search"><input id="cp-in" list="cp-list" placeholder="Ticker or company name" aria-label="Find a company" autocomplete="off"><datalist id="cp-list"></datalist><button type="button" class="gm-btn" id="cp-go">Open</button></div>\n<div class="x-chips" id="cp-quick" style="margin-top:8px"></div>\n<p class="gm-note" id="cp-msg" data-source="profiles"></p>\n</div>\n<div id="cp-body">\n<div class="gm-block">\n<h2 class="gm-h2 x-cm-h" id="cp-name">—</h2>\n<p class="gm-note" id="cp-sub"></p>\n<div class="x-tiles" id="cp-tiles" data-source="profiles constituents"></div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-grid2">\n<div class="gm-block">\n<div class="gm-hrow"><h3 class="gm-h3">Five years of prices</h3><span class="gm-seg" id="cp-rel"></span></div>\n<div class="gm-chart" id="cp-px" data-source="profiles etf_weekly"></div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block"><h3 class="gm-h3">About</h3><div id="cp-about" data-source="profiles"></div></div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-grid2">\n<div class="gm-block"><h3 class="gm-h3">Revenue, with consensus ahead</h3><div class="gm-chart" id="cp-rev" data-source="profiles"></div></div>\n<div class="gm-block"><h3 class="gm-h3">Earnings per share, with consensus ahead</h3><div class="gm-chart" id="cp-eps" data-source="profiles"></div></div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block"><h3 class="gm-h3">Margins</h3><div class="gm-chart" id="cp-margins" data-source="profiles"></div></div>\n<div id="cp-full">\n<div class="gm-grid2">\n<div class="gm-block"><h3 class="gm-h3">Revenue by quarter</h3><div class="gm-chart" id="cp-q" data-source="profiles"></div><p class="gm-note" id="cp-q-note"></p></div>\n<div class="gm-block"><h3 class="gm-h3">Balance sheet, latest year</h3><div class="x-tiles" id="cp-bal" data-source="profiles"></div></div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block"><h3 class="gm-h3">Cash flow and shareholder returns</h3><div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-compact" id="cp-cash" data-source="profiles"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div></div>\n<div class="gm-block"><h3 class="gm-h3">Earnings against expectations</h3><p class="gm-note" id="cp-next"></p><div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-compact" id="cp-earn" data-source="profiles"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div></div>\n<div class="gm-grid2">\n<div class="gm-block"><h3 class="gm-h3">Valuation and returns on capital by year</h3><div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-compact" id="cp-km" data-source="profiles"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div></div>\n<div class="gm-block"><h3 class="gm-h3">Consensus estimates</h3><div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-compact" id="cp-est" data-source="profiles"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div></div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-block" id="cp-calls-wrap">\n<h3 class="gm-h3">Earnings-call tone</h3><p class="gm-note" id="cp-calls-note"></p>\n<div class="gm-grid2"><div class="gm-chart" id="cp-calls" data-source="call_scores"></div><div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-compact" id="cp-topics" data-source="call_scores"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div></div>\n</div>\n<div class="gm-grid2">\n<div class="gm-block"><h3 class="gm-h3">Where it sits in the universe</h3><div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-compact" id="cp-funds" data-source="profiles score_history"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div></div>\n<div class="gm-block"><h3 class="gm-h3">Peers</h3><div class="gm-tablewrap"><table class="gm-table gm-compact" id="cp-peers" data-source="profiles constituents"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</template>'};for(const t of Object.keys(e)){const a=document.getElementById(t);a&&!a.firstElementChild&&(a.innerHTML=e[t])}})();
